Toyota to open 4th showroom in early 2014
Phnom Penh: Toyota Cambodia, the exclusive distributor of Toyota in Cambodia, recently completed the construction of its fourth showroom in Phnom Penh and is in the process of launching a test car before it officially opens. Worked in February 2014.
The showroom is located along Monivong Blvd, near the Bokor traffic light, built on a total area of 700 square meters at a cost of about $ 500,000 alone.
Kong Nuon, president of Toyota Cambodia, confirmed to the Post yesterday that the opening of the new showroom was part of the company's long-term investment plan and to make it easier to locate cars. To customers.
According to car market experts, demand for cars in Cambodia is growing year by year, amid growing middle-class households.
The new and used Toyota market has the largest share, accounting for 90% of the total market share.
Last August, Toyota Cambodia also tried to import high-quality used Toyota cars for sale after seeing an increase in demand for the car.
A representative for Toyota Cambodia said sales of new Toyota vehicles in 2013 totaled about 1,000 units, up 25 percent from a year earlier.
$ 200 million investment in pig farms
Phnom Penh: Mr. Mong Reththy, a millionaire and CPP senator, has partnered with a state-owned Chinese company from Guangzhou to jointly invest $ 200 million in expanding pig farms in Cambodia in response to Market demand and prevent large imports from neighboring countries.
Mong Reththy told the Post on Sunday that he had already signed a memorandum of understanding (MoU) with a Chinese company on Thursday.
They will invest $ 200 million with us, but they will give us money step by step.
He says under the agreement he plans to raise at least 300,000 pigs a year.
Srun Pov, president of the Cambodian Pig Farmers Association, welcomed the investment as a contribution to imports.
According to Srun Pov, the government has set a quota for importing about 800 pigs a day.
He said Cambodia consumes about 4,000 pigs a day across the country.
Mong Reththy bought and imported Yorkshire pigs from the United Kingdom in 2009.
The import is estimated at $ 35 million.
This estimate does not include unchecked imports from Thailand and Vietnam.
Mong Reththy says he will build a new pig farm 10 kilometers from the old site in Sihanoukville's Kampong Seila district on 50 hectares.
He said more construction would be needed to achieve this goal.
Mr. Srun Pov said the investment was still limited in response to growing demand.
Cambodia consumes only 1 million pigs per year, or one person eats between 5 and 7 kg of pork per year, but in fact, when GDP and double-digit growth, each person needs 15 kg of pork per One year or 2.1 million pigs per year.
Cashew Center is helping to increase revenue
Kampong Thom: The cashew peeling center in Ta Theav village, Kampong Svay district, Kampong Thom province is playing an important role in improving the livelihood of local people.
The center was launched in May 2013 with the assistance of the Asian Development Bank.
The center operates through an association among cashew farmers.
The lack of a local cashew processing and processing plant has been a major challenge in the development of the cashew industry and has prevented farmers from taking full advantage of the crop's potential.
Oum On, president of the Prasat Sambo Cashew Nut Association, said this was a solution to avoid relying on selling cashews to brokers, who generally lower prices.
In the past, cashew farmers in the association could sell fresh cashew nuts to traders for $ 1.5 per kilogram, but now they can sell finished cashews for $ 12 per kilogram.
The new center is operating through the Prasat Sambor Cashew Association.
However, the center is still able to buy and produce cashews on a small scale, only collecting cashews from members of the association, who are all cashew producers.
The association has 383 families from nine villages in two districts, Kampong Svay and Sambo.
Ros Sothea, public relations officer for the Asian Development Bank, said the purpose of the center was to inspire people to do business in addition to their day-to-day business, which is largely dependent on farming.
The addition is to help them move out of poverty, as the poverty rate in the region is high at 30 to 40 percent.
Chan Ry, a farmer with 4 hectares of cashew plantations, said that the operation of the peeling center had helped the family to a certain extent because she was able to sell crops at a higher price and have more work to increase income.
A report released in 2010 on the vision of the Cambodian cashew industry, conducted by the International Finance Corporation, a member of the World Development Bank, shows that Cambodia is the 10th largest producer of fresh cashew nuts in the world. About 60,000 tonnes of cashews a year, more than the Philippines at 5,000 tonnes and Sri Lanka at 5,500 tonnes, while both countries have peeling and processing plants.
Therefore, cashew production in Cambodia is enough to set up a processing plant.
Tourism services are preparing to receive tourists at the end of the year
Restaurant and hoteliers in Cambodia are preparing to welcome guests on the eve of the upcoming New Year.
An increase in foreign tourists is expected to visit Cambodia this holiday season.
This is what those in the restaurant and hotel industry told the Post yesterday.
Lou Meng, president of the Cambodian Hotel Association and owner of the Almin Hotel, said there had been an increase in bookings for both food and accommodation compared to last year.
Apparently, his hotel is already fully booked.
The programs he is referring to include decorating the hotel with Christmas trees, offering discounts, raffle giveaways, concerts and banquets.
The guests he received were from Europe, Asia and Cambodian expatriates returning to visit Cambodia.
Owning Almin Hotel and several restaurants in Phnom Penh, Lou Meng is also preparing a number of New Year's Eve greetings.
According to Ho Vandy, co-chair of the Tourism Working Group, demonstrations related to domestic politics have had little effect on foreign visitors to Cambodia.
The package includes discounts for group guests, New Year's Eve excursions, special gifts and dinner.
This package is to attract more foreign visitors to visit Cambodia.
Most of our guests are from Europe, France, Australia, China and Korea.
Mr. Tith Chantha, Director General of the General Department of the Ministry of Tourism, confirmed that the Ministry of Tourism has not organized any special program to attract foreign tourists in this New Year.
However, he continued:
It has already helped attract tourists.
Data from the Ministry of Tourism shows that in the first 10 months of 2013, 3.5 million foreign tourists visited Cambodia, an increase of 10.5 percent from the previous year.
Nine mobile phone companies plan to form telecom operators association
Cambodia's telecoms operator TRC plans to set up an independent body to discuss issues in the industry, according to a statement issued yesterday by the Cambodian Telecommunications Regulator (TRC).
The establishment of the Telecommunications Operators Association of Cambodia (ATC) was agreed in principle at a meeting with the TRC on December 13, the statement said.
The new association is described as providing a single voice for discussion and to improve dialogue with the TRC.
Although ATC will push for discussions between rivals on issues such as pricing, technology changes and Cambodia's telecommunications law, Hundt said it could not resolve all industry disputes.
In the end, we are all competitors.
He stressed that the institution was still in the planning stage and there were no meeting initiatives or deadlines set.
In the same press release, the regulator said that the nine operators agreed to comply with and comply with government regulations related to Prakas No. 232 in compliance with the fixed price of 4.5 cents per phone. Minutes and offline 5.95 cents per minute.
On November 28, the government reintroduced the minimum rate, more than three years after the law was drafted to ensure fair competition.
The announcement drew strong opposition from mobile phone users and telecom operators earlier this month.
Yesterday, two telecom operators, including Smart, were found to be offering a 100 per cent top-up bonus as part of a holiday promotion that appears to contradict Proclamation 232. .
But Hundt said the increase was in line with regulatory rules allowing publicity during the holidays.
Our bonus offer is in line with this provision.
Mao Chakrya, director general of the TRC, said the regulator would hold an internal meeting on Monday to review the industry's situation and minimum prices.
Cambodia receives more than 4 million tons of rice
Preliminary data from the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries shows that Cambodia harvested more than 4 million tonnes of rice by December 11 during this year's harvest.
Khem Chenda, director of the Ministry of Agriculture's administrative affairs department, told the Post that this was a preliminary harvest that had not yet been completed.
But with government policy, we have to do preliminary data for the whole year first.
He said the harvest season runs from August to the end of February next year.
On the other hand, we are still happy with the provisional results.
I have observed that we will get good results this year, although we have had floods that have affected some areas, but it is not serious.
We met with provincial officials and farmers after the floods, they said the situation was better than last year.
So far, he said, he has bought about 10,000 tonnes of fragrant rice from farmers in Pursat, Battambang and Banteay Meanchey provinces for milling for export.
Khem Chenda hopes that this year's results will be higher as farmers change their seeds to the good ones the ministry has set for the 10 varieties to achieve higher yields.
Data from the Ministry of Agriculture shows that Cambodia produced about 9.29 million tonnes of paddy for the dry and wet seasons, with a surplus of 4.7 tonnes.
With three options for a doubling of garment workers' wages to $ 160 by 2018, the Garment Manufacturers Association of Cambodia has opted for a fourth option, simply raising wages to $ 130.
In a letter sent by the Garment Manufacturers Association to Labor Minister Ith Sam Heng on Wednesday, the Post said it would agree to a 10-fold increase in the minimum wage. Dollars every year for the next five years, while the current minimum wage for workers is only $ 80.
The letter, sent on Tuesday, the same day that the Ministry of Labor team, including union representatives and the Garment Manufacturers Association of Cambodia, outlined three different plans to raise wages to $ 160 by 2018. .
On Tuesday, the working group asked the government to increase wages over a five-year period, adding $ 16 annually until 2018, based on factors such as economic inflation or raising the minimum wage to $ 160 next year.
Ken Loo, secretary general of the Garment Manufacturers Association, said that while the association's figures differed from those of the working group, it was not their position.
Loo said on Wednesday:
After agreeing to pay less than half of the minimum wage proposed by the working group, the letter went on to ask the government to implement a zero-tolerance policy on illegal strikes and to discuss and review the draft law. Union as soon as possible.
The statement called on the government to enforce the law.
Another letter, signed by the Garment Manufacturers Association of Cambodia, is a memorandum of understanding signed by government officials and the International Labor Organization that allows the Better Factories Cambodia program to continue working in Cambodia for three years. More.
The Better Factories Cambodia program announced in October its plans to launch a portion of its factory inspection report.
Independent union leaders met recently and rallied to demand an increase in the minimum wage to at least $ 154 in 2014. The workers' current minimum wage is $ 80.
Cambodia attracts least for regional investment
New research shows that Cambodia is the least attractive destination for investment among ASEAN countries.
A 2013 ASEAN Competitiveness Survey, released last week, ranks Cambodia one point below Brunei in terms of investor attractiveness.
The survey found that only 21 percent of the 502 respondents to the survey, all of them from the ASEAN business community, found that Cambodia was a country worthy of investment at the time. The future.
At the same time, more than 40 percent said they intend to invest in Singapore, Malaysia, Indonesia or Thailand.
As a result, Cambodia has an overall attractiveness score of 4.06 out of 10 points, beating only Brunei 3.67.
Conducted by the ASEAN Business Advisory Council, the survey included responses from only three companies in Cambodia, with 72 per cent of the responses coming from Thailand and Vietnam.
The council is a working group set up in 2003 by representatives from ASEAN countries to provide private sector feedback on the region's economic integration.
Mey Kalyan said that while Laos is a strong source of energy resources in the region, Cambodia's free investment regulations allow foreigners to own 100 percent of the stock, lower labor costs and stagnant political stability. Contact
These are important cards for investing.
It may be that labor costs in Myanmar are lower, but you have to think about equality for workers and your level of productivity.
The ASEAN survey also found that Cambodia's foreign direct investment (FDI) activity or export trade was among the least attractive to investors, at just 3.3 per cent, equal to Thailand.
Among the respondents in the survey named Cambodia as an export destination planned for the next three years.
But more than 12 per cent found Myanmar and 3.9 per cent identified Laos as a planned export destination for the next three years.
As ASEAN competition intensifies, companies will make decisions based on transportation, energy costs, customs processes and nearby supply chains, but also rely on available skills.
Cambodia and Myanmar visa waiver
The visa waiver agreement was signed last Thursday.
This is according to a spokesman for the Cambodian Ministry of Foreign Affairs.
The visa waiver agreement between Cambodia and Myanmar will take effect on January 12 next year, meaning ordinary passport holders will be able to enter and leave the country without a visa. Can continue to stay up to 14 days.
Koy Kuong, a spokesman for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, told the Post yesterday:
The second is to increase tourism between the two countries.
Third is the people-to-people exchanges leading to the 2015 ASEAN Community.
The visa waiver agreement aims to facilitate travel between people in the two countries and at the same time boost tourism.
For Cambodia, Myanmar is the last ASEAN member country to sign the visa waiver agreement.
Myanmar Airways International launched flights to Cambodia in December 2012.
Myanmar Airways International has suspended its flights to Cambodia for about three months due to declining demand.
He says it is an emerging market as Myanmar has the potential to not only attract tourists from Cambodia but also attract Cambodian traders who wish to invest and expand more branches in the country. .
However, he wants Myanmar to increase the number of affordable hotels because the cost of accommodation in Myanmar is so high.
The number of tourists from Myanmar to Cambodia increased by about 12 per cent in the first eight months of this year compared to the same period last year.
However, its market share to Cambodia is low at only 0.1 per cent compared to other ASEAN members.
Lao Heng noted that Myanmar tourists like to shop for high-priced brands.
They choose to visit Thailand and Singapore, while Cambodia does not have luxury and branded shopping centers.
To attract Myanmar tourists to Cambodia, Lao Heng proposed the construction of more modern shopping malls and the sale of branded luxury goods and products.
ACLEDA tightens security measures
