Israeli companies interested in greenhouse farming in Cambodia
An Israeli agricultural company is seeking access to Cambodia's agricultural market through the introduction of greenhouse technology, a new concept in Cambodia.
The project is to provide greenhouse construction services from 1,000 to 3,000 square meters to a group of six or more farmers.
In addition to this package, construction management services have an additional $ 4,000.
Direct or online agricultural consulting services cost about $ 10,000, bringing the total cost to build and maintain the greenhouse to $ 50,000.
EAS provides all the support and helps farmers increase their annual fruit and vegetable yields and provide better markets for their products.
Speaking at the opening ceremony of the seminar on green agriculture, ecotourism and greenhouse cities, Deputy Director General of the General Department of Agriculture, Ms. Chan Phal Loeun, said that vegetable products in Cambodia have not yet met the needs of eating enough. Imports from Vietnam and Thailand are required because vegetable growing techniques such as water management by Cambodian farmers are still limited.
She expects that the introduction of greenhouse technology will be one way to take Cambodia's agricultural sector to the next level, which will help reduce the import of vegetables into the Cambodian market.
SNEC Supreme Advisor to the National Economic Council Mey Kalyan believes that the introduction of greenhouse technology is not suitable for Cambodian farmers who lack the capital to pay for the installation of greenhouses.
He worries that farmers could fall into debt if they borrow money from financial institutions to buy technology as well as build greenhouses.
To increase vegetable production, Mey Kalyan suggested that farmers should adopt appropriate technologies, refer to existing methods and continue to improve.
He said that technology that is too high while the capacity and knowledge is limited is a risk for practitioners.
According to the total land area plan, the company plans to select one of three provinces: Pailin, Banteay Meanchey and Siem Reap.
Japan pledges $ 134 million loan to Cambodia
The Japanese government has pledged about $ 134 million in new loans for three major infrastructure projects in Cambodia.
The agreement was reached at a joint press conference between Prime Minister Hun Sen and Japanese Prime Minister Shinzo Abe on Sunday in Tokyo, culminating in a bilateral meeting held on the sidelines of the summit. Commemorating the 40th ASEAN-Japan Anniversary and the 5th Mekong-Japan Summit.
According to Japanese media reports, according to information from the Japanese Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Sunday's meeting with Prime Minister Hun Sen, Abe promised a new loan of about 13.8 billion yen (134 million US dollars). For three infrastructure projects in Cambodia.
The rest of the money will cover more projects to improve irrigation and connect highways from Phnom Penh to Thailand.
The media added that Abe and Hun Sen had also agreed to begin negotiations on a bilateral aviation agreement to launch direct flights between the two countries.
According to the Japanese Embassy in Cambodia, since 1992, more than $ 2 billion in Japanese loans and grants have been provided to Cambodia.
According to the Council for the Development of Cambodia, between 1994 and October this year, the Cambodian government approved $ 593 million in foreign investment from Japan.
In 2012, Cambodia approved a $ 271 million Japanese investment project.
A new Cambodian rice exporter announced yesterday that it would partner with a Chinese company to invest $ 100 million to buy fragrant rice in Cambodia and export 200,000 tonnes a year to the Chinese market.
Confirmation of this amount is considered impossible by Cambodian exporters.
At present, all Cambodian rice exporters combined can export around 350,000 tonnes of rice this year.
