Co-operative company promotes organic rice
Mekong Orissa Rice Exporters yesterday signed a joint venture agreement with CEDAC to jointly strengthen Cambodia's organic rice production and export to international markets as demand for this type of rice grows.
Through this contract, Mekong Orissa will be one of the first companies to help buy organic rice from farmers and export it to international markets.
In the past, only CEDAC tried to cultivate the idea and encourage farmers to produce chemical-free rice.
Speaking at the signing ceremony of the cooperation agreement held at the office of the Rural Development Bank yesterday, Hun Lak, CEO of Orissa Trading Company, said that Cambodian fragrant rice was receiving more orders from importers. Rice, and in addition, they also asked to buy organic rice from Cambodia.
Therefore, it is a good opportunity for us to be prepared to strengthen production and take advantage of the potential of organic rice production as well.
Organic rice will provide opportunities for communities and farmers to get more profits.
He said that many rice-producing countries in the region could not easily turn to increasing organic rice production like Cambodia, as the rate of use of chemicals to increase yields in rice crops was much higher.
Organic rice production requires great care and diligence, as farmers must avoid the use of chemical fertilizers and pesticides to kill pests, and all production processes from start to finish must be done. Up with the right rules that pass the inspection and have a clear certificate.
Through this agreement, Mekong Orissa Trading Co. will jointly provide capital to increase the production of fragrant rice and explore potential markets for high-quality organic rice.
CEDAC is responsible for providing organic rice production techniques to farmers and continues to monitor and evaluate to ensure that farmers' organic rice production processes are carried out in accordance with the rules and procedures in accordance with organic conditions or not?
