Cambodia lags behind in energy
Low electricity supplies and reliance on Cambodia's oil imports have put the country low on the World Economic Forum's 2014 World Energy Index report.
Cambodia ranks 120th out of 124 countries in the EAPI Energy Architecture Performance Index with a score of 0.36.
While Myanmar and Laos are not included in the index due to lack of data.
Cambodia ranks last among ASEAN member countries.
The report says:
Cambodia leads Tanzania, Benin, Lebanon and Yemen.
Norway ranks among the 124 countries with an EAPI score of 0.7.
The highest score is 1.
Srey Chanthy, president of the Cambodian Economic Association, cited a lack of diversification in the power sector as one of the reasons for the low rankings.
Data from the Ministry of Commerce shows that Cambodia imported oil from Singapore, Thailand and Vietnam, and in the first 10 months of this year spent $ 1.2 billion on oil imports.
In May, the government released a draft energy efficiency policy developed with the EU Energy Initiative Dialogue, which calls for a 20 percent reduction in energy consumption by 2035, resulting in Estimates could save $ 320 million.
Rubber control law causes dissatisfaction
To strengthen the quality and reputation of Cambodian rubber production, the General Department of Rubber of the Ministry of Agriculture is preparing a draft law governing rubber to ensure quality and standards.
Some of the provisions in the draft law will encourage rubber producers to adhere to production principles in accordance with standard techniques that require more complex production processes, and this has led some growers and exporters to worry about surface pollution. Rubber plantations are declining.
Ly Phalla, director general of the General Department of Rubber of Cambodia, told the Post yesterday that the drafting of a law on rubber in Cambodia was to ensure the sustainability of the rubber sector and to achieve greater efficiency in the development of past production. Rubber is still anarchic, as in the case of many farmers who buy substandard rubber from rubber seed producers, while some rubber processing companies do not meet government standards.
Among the provisions of the draft law, there are a number of important new provisions, such as requiring the owners of rubber plantations and nurseries to have a permit to register rubber plantations and to apply for a certificate of quality. Yes.
All operations must be carried out under the supervision and evaluation of the competent officials, and any re-establishment or relocation of rubber plantations and nurseries must apply for a permit for registration and re-certification of plantations.
The processed rubber products to be exported must have a certificate of origin and an export permit issued by the Rubber Authority and a certificate of quality of processed rubber products from a laboratory with an official certificate.
Lim Heng, deputy general manager of An Madi Group, which owns several hectares of rubber plantations, said some of the provisions in the draft law were too restrictive.
Data from the General Department of Rubber shows that so far Cambodia has a total of 320,000 hectares of rubber plantations, of which 130,000 hectares can be planted with rubber.
Mak Kimhong, president of the Cambodian Rubber Association, supported the law.
Demand for organic rice rises, but production remains challenging
Demand for organic rice in the international market is growing and Cambodia is receiving increasing orders.
According to CEDAC data, Cambodia's organic rice exports this year are 320 tonnes, up from 150 tonnes in 2012.
But agricultural experts say Cambodia still faces the challenge of taking full advantage of the trend, as many farmers are unable to produce according to market demand.
CEDAC President Yang Saing Koma told the Post yesterday that demand for organic rice in major countries such as the European Union, the United States and Asia is growing, which is a good thing for Cambodian farmers to increase production income. Rice.
But not many farmers can produce organic rice properly to benefit from the growing market demand.
He said that growing organic rice requires a lot of care and diligence, because farmers must absolutely avoid the use of chemical fertilizers and pesticides to kill pests, and all production processes from the beginning to the harvest must be done. With proper rules, go through inspection and have a clear certificate.
CEDAC has been encouraging farmers to practice organic farming methods since 2004, and so far only 5,000 families have practiced organic farming properly and those who have practiced for more than three years There are only more than 1,000 families.
Dom Don, a farmer living in Prasat village, Moha Saing commune, Phnom Sruoch district, Kampong Speu province, has been producing organic rice since 2006 and he observed that organic rice is in high demand in the market and the price is higher than regular rice.
During this year's harvest, Dum Don can sell organic fragrant rice at 1,650 riel per kilogram, while regular fragrant rice is only 1,200 riel per kilogram.
According to experts, Cambodia has the potential to build a reputation for producing and exporting organic fragrant rice due to the low rate of use of chemicals to increase rice yields compared to neighboring countries.
Srey Chanthy, president of the Cambodian Association of Economists and an agricultural expert, reminded that if Cambodia wants to become an important country in the production and export of organic rice, Cambodia must establish a trustworthy institutional structure to certify Quality and standard of organic products to increase customer confidence in the market.
If we are truly willing and able to overcome those obstacles, we will benefit greatly.
