Cambodia-Japan Visa Agreement Promotes Investment in Cambodia
Phnom Penh: Visa regulations between Cambodia and Japan have been relaxed in an effort to boost Japanese investment in Cambodia.
A statement issued by the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation on Monday said that the agreement, which entered into force yesterday, would allow holders of ordinary passports in Japan and Cambodia to be eligible for long-term and multiple entry visas. Times for both countries are valid for up to 3 years.
Holders of diplomatic passports will be automatically issued visas within 30 days in Cambodia and Japan.
Representatives from both countries will announce the visa change at the ASEAN-Japan Summit on December 13 in Tokyo.
Koy Kuong, a spokesman for the Cambodian Ministry of Foreign Affairs, said the criteria for applying for long-term and multiple-entry visas remained unclear and had been decided independently by both countries.
The announcement comes a week after the Council for the Development of Cambodia and the ASEAN-Japan Center held a series of seminars to promote Japanese investment in Cambodia.
CDC statistics show that Japanese investment from 1994 to October 2013, excluding investment in Cambodia's 27 special economic zones, totaled $ 387 million.
Japanese foreign investment increased from 2012 to a total of $ 270.9 million.
But that number dropped to $ 20 million and $ 32.9 million for investment in special economic zones in October 2013.
Ang Kim Eang, president of the Cambodian Association of Travel Agents, said the new visa was an important factor in boosting tourism between the two countries.
He called on the two governments to reduce visa bureaucracy for the convenience of travelers and for airline operators to display regular weekly flights between the two countries.
With the new agreement, airline operators should step up the process of capturing domestic flights.
Figures from the Ministry of Tourism show that Japanese tourists accounted for 5 percent of total foreign tourist arrivals in Cambodia between January and October this year, the highest increase.
