Seafood business not as expected
Kep: Traders at Kep Beach Crab Market originally expected their sales to be more profitable than the usual three-day Sea Festival holiday, but things did not go as planned. No hope.
When customers pass by, Leh Maria always asks the group to come in to help buy their squid shrimp, saying that the seafood is cheap!
But this expectation failed.
Maria says:
She bought 100 kilograms of squid and 50 kilograms of shrimp, hoping that the festival would attract at least a million people and that her business would be successful.
The Crab Market, located along the coast of Kep, is a major tourist attraction.
Visitors often take a break to eat, buy fresh seafood, have lunch or dinner.
Some vendors cited local authorities blocking roads along Kep Crab Market, making it impossible for tourists with cars to shop, while others with regular businesses The crab market claims that it is because there are too many new sellers.
The Sea Festival was held in Kep from December 6 to 8, in line with several international meetings held in the nearby province of Kampot.
Meetings such as the World's Most Beautiful Beach Club Meeting, the Tourism Ministers' Meeting of Cambodia, Laos, Myanmar and Vietnam, and the Greater Mekong Subregion Tourism Summit were all held last weekend.
Expecting the Sea Festival to bring more tourists to the province, hundreds of new vendors at Kep Crab Market also appeared at the time.
On the other hand, there are not many customers as the road along Crab Market is not allowed to travel by car or tuk tuk on Saturday, which is the most important day of the festival.
Vendor Khim Khim added that crabs were the same price and even cheaper on weekends, but not as many visitors.
Almost every trader who sells at the Crab Market is disappointed.
Another squid and shrimp trader, 28-year-old Ser Sary, came to sell for the first time at the festival, but lost business.
She bought shrimp for 25,000 riel per kilogram to sell, but she could only sell for 15,000 riel per kilogram because there were more sellers and fewer buyers.
Seafood prices vary from place to place in the same market.
Many new traders who do not have a job in the market regularly decide to sell their goods at a lower price to get a refund.
Sorn Sokhon, 34, has been selling crabs in the market for eight years.
On a normal day, she said, there were only about 30 crab sellers at the market, but these days there have been a few hundred more.
Many new sellers expect to make a lot of money, but the time is not right and they are making us lose money.
He can sell for an average of $ 500 a day during the regular weekends, but this Sea Festival he can only sell for $ 200 a day.
Som Chenda, director of the Kep provincial tourism department, told the Post that the festival attracted at least 150,000 tourists, including about 3,000 foreigners.
He added that it was necessary for authorities to ban tuk-tuks or other means of transportation on the main road in order to maintain order during the festival.
Vendors always think about profit and want to have more guests, but they also have to think about the difficulties of the organizers.
Although some places are not selling well, many others are benefiting from the sea festival.
