Sok Chenda Sophea, secretary-general of the Council for the Development of Cambodia (CDC), told reporters after a meeting on Cambodia moving forward with plans for growth and investment at the Le Royal Hotel yesterday that the CDC was drafting an investment law. It is scheduled to be completed soon for submission to the Council of Ministers for approval and implementation in 2014.
He claimed that in the past, Myanmar was not very attractive to investors because it was surrounded by economic sanctions, but after many reforms, the country has become an attractive place for investors. More.
It is time for us to clean up, improve and adapt to the new situation.
He said that if Cambodia wants to develop industry and small and medium enterprises (SMEs), Cambodia must create laws that facilitate the growth of industry and SME.
The Investment Law in Cambodia was first passed in 1994, then amended for the first time in 2003.
However, CDC representatives have not been able to elaborate on what needs to be changed or innovated, saying only that it will be more conducive to the private sector by reducing complicated procedures. Strengthen the development of skilled labor, facilitate private companies to get easy credit for the development goals of agriculture, industry and SME.
The fifth-term government, led by the CPP, has been controversial as the opposition remains dissatisfied with the election results and has begun to gradually reform some state institutions.
On November 13, the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia issued guidelines on strengthening the effectiveness of customs clearance inspections.
Earlier this week, Minister of Commerce Sun Chanthol said that the Ministry of Commerce had signed a number of prakas to eliminate procedures that had previously complicated the business environment and continued to make improvements. More.
During yesterday's meeting, a group of traders representing large private companies raised challenges related to the mismatch between the supply of labor and the actual needs of companies.
Strengthening human resources, skilled labor is also a necessary factor.
Martin McCarthy, CEO of Total Cambodia Petroleum, said:
Finding potential young engineers, mechanical technicians, construction engineers is still a big challenge.
Cambodian youth face unemployment
The drop-out rate in Cambodia is a worrying sign for the country's future, as young Cambodians will become more involved in the country's economic development in the future, according to a report.
New data released yesterday by the National Institute of Statistics and the International Labor Organization (ILO) found that the high dropout rate was a worrying sign for the nation's future.
Figures from the Cambodian Child Labor and Labor Organization survey show that in 2012, about 3.8 percent of young people, or 80,980 out of more than two million young people between the ages of 15 and 24, were currently unemployed.
A report released yesterday details the low level of education of young Cambodians in the labor force.
Only 46.7 percent of those employed young people have completed secondary school, while only 41.5 percent have completed primary school.
Mey Kalyan, a senior adviser to the Supreme National Economic Council, said recovering from a history of conflict and poverty balancing education and employment was a challenge.
He outlined the government's rectangular strategy and it focused on human resources.
Rong Chhun, president of the Cambodian Independent Teachers Association, linked dropout rates to poverty.
Hong Choeun, director of the government's National Employment Agency, said yesterday that as Cambodia shifted from labor-intensive jobs to the middle-skilled workforce, the education system was struggling with the volatile market.
Officials worried about tourism impact
Government officials have expressed concern about the impact on tourism as the opposition party plans to hold a demonstration on Human Rights Day next month in Siem Reap.
Minister of Tourism Thong Khon told the Post that the CNRP's December 10 demonstration could affect the business of business people and people living in Siem Reap if it does not go as planned. Stated in the law.
Across the country, the first large number of Vietnamese visitors visit Cambodia.
The CNRP immediately refused to recognize the results of the July 28 election, citing irregularities on election day, and called for the establishment of an independent commission to look into the matter. Really out of those anomalies.
Since then, the opposition party has staged several demonstrations in Phnom Penh.
Responding to concerns that the demonstration would scare tourists away from Cambodia, Ke Sovannarot, head of the CNRP's working group in Siem Reap, said there was nothing to worry about. The demonstration will be non-violent.
If the authorities and the police do not show violence or muscle to the people and the protesters, the protest will take place without any violence at all.
She said it was the authorities' display of violence that frightened tourists.
Ho Vandy, co-chair of the Tourism Working Group, said the private sector was concerned about the news of the event, but could not confirm the exact extent of the impact until it was clear. From the specific location that will be organized from this event first.
He called on the two sides to negotiate to find a political solution to avoid any negative impact on the business environment.
Siem Reap is a major tourist destination in Cambodia, attracting millions of tourists every year.
Visitors to Siem Reap reached 1.7 million in the first 10 months of this year.
So far, the CNRP has not yet determined the location of the demonstration, but according to Thong Khon, the rally is likely to take place in front of the Angkor Wat temple.
This is the first visit since she took office in July 2011 and after she missed out on the East Asia Summit in November 2012 in Cambodia. Host the ASEAN Summit.
This is her trip to Asia, she will go to Seoul, South Korea from December 4 to 5, then to Nay Pyi Taw, Yangon, Myanmar from December 6 to 7. December.
During her visit to Cambodia, she is expected to meet with King Norodom Sihamoni, Prime Minister Hun Sen, Deputy Prime Minister Keat Chhon and other senior government officials.
According to an IMF press release, she will address students at the Royal School of Administration and exchange views with women business leaders and policymakers, as well as consultants and private sector representatives.
I will visit all three countries at different stages.
Korea is the world's 13th largest economy, Cambodia has growing economic potential, and Myanmar is one of the countries that is getting the most attention.
Cambodia, Hong Kong seek trade boost
More than 100 Hong Kong and Cambodian business delegations met at a meeting in Phnom Penh yesterday to seek opportunities to boost trade between the two sides.
Benjamin Zhao, deputy director of the Hong Kong Business Development Council (HKTDC), who led a Hong Kong business delegation to Cambodia, said Hong Kong businessmen were seeing an increase in Cambodia-Hong Kong trade through the exchange of goods and services. Services in the past, so the two sides should understand each other more to cooperate more.
In the first nine months of 2013, bilateral trade between Cambodia and Hong Kong totaled $ 762 million, up 19 percent from the same period last year.
Of that, Hong Kong's exports to Cambodia were more than $ 685 million, while Cambodia's exports to Hong Kong were more than $ 76 million.
Lim Heng, vice-president of the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce, urged Hong Kong businessmen to consider setting up their representative offices in Cambodia to facilitate the gathering of information on doing business in Cambodia and finding potential investment partners.
Following a year-long warning, the European Commission on Tuesday called on EU members to ban fish imports from Cambodia, saying the country had not taken action against illegal fishing.
A spokesman for the European Commission's press department said on Tuesday that foreign fishing vessels were illegally using the Cambodian flag after easily obtaining the country's flag.
Despite being bound by international law, many fishermen are not monitored by Cambodian authorities.
The spokesman said:
Nao Thuok, director general of the Fisheries Administration at the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, said he was unaware of the ban proposed by the European Commission, but said that due to strict standards, Cambodia did not export fish. To the EU.
Despite no exports to Europe, a European Commission spokesman said ships carrying Cambodian flags in international waters would be banned from selling fish to the EU. Once the request is approved.
The ban will take effect in February.
The ministry will work harder with the private sector
The Cambodian Ministry of Commerce said yesterday that it would increase cooperation with the private sector through better trade facilitation and eliminate informal costs on export and import procedures. Enter to strengthen the competitiveness of the Cambodian business sector.
As part of a plan to improve the short-term business environment, Mr. Chanthol mentioned the signing of a prakas to repeal the old procedure that requires exporters to apply for a CO certificate of origin before exporting. Although the country to be imported is not required.
He signed off on repealing the procedure, which requires exporters to apply for a one-year GSP certification, but it is still valid.
The Ministry of Commerce has also decided to reduce the membership fee of GMAC, a member of the Garment Manufacturers Association of Cambodia, by 20 per cent from the original $ 600 per year to boost investment in the sector.
The establishment of an up-to-date information center where traders can call in to provide advice and provide information related to informal payments to ministry officials is also a Ministry of Commerce plan to implement in the short term.
GMAC President Van Sou Ieng welcomed the improvement in some of the procedures proposed by the Ministry of Commerce and hoped it would benefit the garment sector and other exporters.
CNRP lawmaker Son Chhay said that in the past, applying for business licenses and importing and exporting documents from the Ministry of Commerce was a major obstacle for traders because of these procedures. Up with bribes to ministry officials and complicated.
Corruption in the Ministry of Commerce in the past is a serious problem that requires a clear strategy to address.
Ho Vandy, co-chair of the tourism working group, said that if the ministry's reforms were true, positive developments would be seen in the next six months or a year, but if the reforms were enough. But it will fail even more.
Vegetarian business in Phnom Penh is booming
Phnom Penh: In recent years, the vegetarian business has been gaining momentum in both local and foreign restaurants, as the number of tourists to Cambodia has been increasing year by year, which is an important factor in driving Food service in Cambodia.
Vegetarian restaurants are also an attractive food service for foreign tourists, which is on the rise.
Panareth, who runs the restaurant on behalf of the Malaysian owner, who travels to and from Cambodia, said the challenge for vegetarian restaurants is to create new dishes to suit their tastes.
Initially, a lot of money was spent on promoting the restaurant to customers.
The promotion lasted for two years until more and more customers became aware of the restaurant.
At first we targeted Cambodian customers, but most of the people who came to our shop were foreigners, not Cambodians.
We are also starting to change direction.
Most of the customers are foreigners living and working in Phnom Penh, vegetarians and some Cambodians.
Currently, the restaurant has nearly 100 dishes from different countries such as Thailand, Vietnam, Malaysia, Mexico and Italy.
She added that new dishes have to go through several taste tests before being released on the menu:
Here we focus only on enhancing the taste of food.
For a restaurant business, taste is important.
Efforts in advertising, flexibility and creativity have made the restaurant stand out in the market while the number of vegetarian customers in Cambodia is still limited.
Rising commodity prices are threatening workers
Garment factory workers in Cambodia are facing another level of hardship in their daily lives due to higher spending on daily necessities after the rise of almost all major commodities in the market since the beginning of the month. This November is coming.
Economists say rising daily commodity prices will put workers at greater risk while wages remain low.
Kim Rina, a 32-year-old worker at the Hoyia Garment Factory located along National Road 2 in Chak Angre Leu commune, told the Post on Monday that she had to pay more for her daily living. Since the beginning of this month, the prices of all necessities have risen sharply while incomes have remained the same.
Many garment workers working in factories in Phnom Penh say they are struggling to make ends meet as rising market prices.
Many of the main items that factory workers use for daily living, including vegetables, rice, spices, bean curd, sugar, salt, oil and fat, have risen by around 20 percent since the beginning of November.
The garment industry has been a major source of revenue for Cambodia so far.
The industry employs about 600,000 workers, most of them women.
But income is still not enough to make a decent living, so demands for higher wages in the industry are often made.
In addition to the rising cost of daily necessities, the worker also cited higher spending on rented rooms, rising water and electricity prices.
Vendors of food and other goods for factory workers also struggled because sales were lower.
Ek Rath, a vegetable seller at a market near the Hoya garment factory, said sales were starting to be more difficult due to higher prices and lower sales.
Many vegetables, such as kale, cabbage, tomatoes, imported from Vietnam, rose by about 15 percent from normal prices, while other locally produced vegetables also rose.
Imported goods have risen sharply after the government ordered the General Department of Customs and Excise to strengthen customs revenue collection and strengthen customs governance.
Rising consumer prices are affecting many low-income Cambodians.
Srey Chanthy, president of the Cambodian Association of Economists, said strengthening and fighting corruption was necessary and that taking into account rising commodity inflation was important in this context.
He said the government should lower import tariffs on key commodities such as salt, cooking oil and sugar and other goods commonly consumed by the poor, and raise tariffs on other products such as electronics. Electronics and computers.
Cambodia can save money from energy
Cambodia can save millions of dollars annually by effectively operating its energy business by reducing electricity prices and importing energy.
Minister of Tourism Thong Khon said at the ADB Energy Efficiency Workshop in Phnom Penh yesterday:
He said reducing energy imports would improve Cambodia's competitiveness by reducing overall costs and improving operational efficiency.
Data from the Ministry of Commerce shows that Cambodia imported oil from Singapore, Thailand and Vietnam, and in the first 10 months of this year spent $ 1.2 billion on oil imports.
Neighboring countries such as Vietnam, Thailand and Laos supply much of their electricity to Cambodia.
As one of the most expensive countries in the world.
Access to electricity is very low.
Eliminate unofficial expenditures to boost exports
Newly-appointed Minister Sun Chanthol said in a meeting with the private sector in Phnom Penh yesterday:
Currently, some countries such as the United States and Japan do not need CO, but all exporters are required to apply.
Mr. Sun Chanthol said:
So from now on, exporters no longer have to come to the Ministry of Commerce, they just have their office, the factory can record the information in the computer, so we will look at the Ministry, then we will issue an announcement Information.
The value of the exporter's CO will be deducted from the exporter's bank so that the CO can be printed anywhere they need to.
He said the ministry needed between six and nine months for programming and testing before it officially began.
Requiring exporters to apply for CO, even if some countries do not, is part of the opportunity for corruption, but the ministry's new move is a step towards reducing informal spending.
It can also reduce factory costs and we do not spend a lot of time preparing documents.
Lim Bunheng, president of Laurent, one of Cambodia's leading rice exporters, also hailed the initiative, saying it would help local exporters reap more benefits.
Now the Minister of Commerce has decided to do so, it is a good measure for us as exporters as it can reduce unnecessary costs that can make exporting a little more expensive so we can Loss of competitive advantage in the global market.
According to Lim Bunheng, it usually takes at least three days to get a CO and to pay the official price.
According to data from the Ministry of Commerce, total exports of goods from Cambodia were worth $ 5.2 billion in the first nine months of this year, up 26 percent from $ 4 billion. $ 1 billion in the same period last year.
Fuel imports fall 2%
Officials and experts say the decline does not mean a slowdown in the production chain of local factories and enterprises.
Bin Mai Mealea, deputy managing director at PTT, said his company had reduced its supply of black oil to factories by 10 percent in the past 10 months, but that demand for jet fuel was high. An increase of 22 percent compared to last year.
The decline in black oil is due to the fact that most factories have switched to electricity.
Electricity purchased from Vietnam or electricity from hydropower in Kirirom and Kamchay.
He said the black oil supplied to factories was between 200 and 300 tonnes a month.
Meng Saktheara, director general of the Ministry of Industry, Mines and Energy, said the biggest fuel consuming sectors were public works and transportation.
He sees the decline in fuel imports as likely to continue if Cambodia implements a strategic plan to reduce its reliance on fossil fuels.
Currently, Cambodia imports all kinds of fuel, including from Vietnam, Singapore and Thailand.
