He said that in about six months, the showroom will start selling cars, spare parts and other services.
He added that with the continuous growth of the market and the gross domestic product (GDP), more and more people will be looking for high-end products such as luxury cars.
A number of well-known car companies are seeking a foothold in Cambodia's small but growing luxury car market.
German luxury carmaker Audi will go on sale in mid-2014.
Domestic products still do not have the ability to seize market opportunities
Many local enterprises are not yet able to supply goods in the market to reduce their dependence on imported goods immediately, despite the rising prices of almost all imported goods. .
According to industry experts.
However, the trend of increasing the price of imported goods has increased the competitiveness of selling goods in the market to local production, which may encourage SME owners in Cambodia to strengthen production for the future.
Mr. Meng Saktheara, Director General of the General Department of Industry of the Ministry of Industry, Mines and Energy, said that the increase in the price of imported goods has benefited local production in increasing production and supply, but the current situation of local enterprises is still insufficient. For becoming an alternative supplier yet.
He said that in Cambodia today, there are a total of more than 500,000 enterprises, of which there are only around 30,000 manufacturing enterprises, while in neighboring countries there are millions of enterprises.
Importers told the Post last weekend that the prices of many imported goods, including food, beverages, clothing and daily necessities, had risen by 15 to 20 per cent since the beginning of November.
Sok Chea, marketing manager of Nguon Heng Ly, a food processing enterprise that produces fish sauce, soy sauce and other spices, told the Post yesterday that as prices of foreign products rose, some Cambodian consumers were turning to them. Local products gradually, but most of them are regular products because they are cheaper.
He added that there are many types of goods that customers still can not buy from local enterprises due to the limited quality of products and the type of goods that can be supplied.
Lin Thorn, general manager of Men Sarun Noodle Factory, which supplies noodles and noodles, said his company was capable of supplying goods to the market if there was more support from consumers.
He claims that the total consumption of noodles in Cambodia is about 400,000 cases per month, of which the noodles from his company have a market share of about 20%.
So in the last few days, he has seen an increase in orders.
Multi-purpose port to start in 2014
The construction of a multi-purpose port in Sihanoukville is set to begin in 2014 and is expected to be completed by the end of 2016 to boost Cambodia's exports.
This is according to the official of Sihanoukville Autonomous Port.
The multi-purpose port will be built on a 3-hectare surface connected to the mainland, which will be located as a warehouse for existing goods and will be divided into two parts that serve the import-export of goods and a location for the supply of food. Arrive at an oil rig in the Cambodian Sea.
The construction of the multi-purpose port will cost about $ 80 million under a loan from the Japanese government.
The new 13.5-meter-deep port will be able to accommodate large cargo ships and carry between 30,000 and 50,000 tons of cargo, while the current port will be able to accommodate only one cargo ship. Only ten thousand tons.
Wasonath, deputy director general of the Sihanoukville Autonomous Port, told the Post that the multi-purpose port was designed to facilitate Cambodia's growing exports of agricultural products, such as acacia wood chips, dried potato chips, especially Cambodian rice.
We see Cambodia exporting more agricultural products and garments.
Kim Savuth, president of the Federation of Cambodian Rice Exporters, praised the construction of the new port, but said the exporters' challenge was transporting goods from Phnom Penh to the port in Sihanoukville.
But the important thing is the cost that will be paid when the rice is transported from the factory in Phnom Penh to the port, such as payment, loading to the scanner and loading to the port.
Mr. Wasonath said the new port project was scheduled to start in 2011 and be completed in 2014, but has been postponed so far to further increase capacity and efficiency.
Phal Pisey, secretary of the SMBC Phnom Penh representative office, told the Post that the agreement was for the start of mutual cooperation, including the support and mobilization of Japanese companies to invest in Cambodia to promote trade between the two countries. Both.
SMBC opened its representative office in Phnom Penh in August 2012.
Ms. Pisey said that currently about 63 companies are already operating at PPSEZ, and more than half of them are Japanese companies.
