According to data from the Council for the Development of Cambodia, Cambodia accepted investment from Japan worth about $ 366 million between 1994 and 2012.
Cambodian Jasmine Rice Ranked No. 1
Phnom Penh: Cambodia's jasmine rice won the world's No. 1 quality rice award in 2013.
The announcement was made yesterday at the World Rice Expo in Hong Kong.
This is an honor that will help enhance the reputation of Cambodian rice in the international market.
This is the second time that Cambodia has won an international award after winning it once in Bali, Indonesia in September last year.
Kim Savuth, president of the Federation of Cambodian Rice Exporters, confirmed that Cambodia's fragrant rice had won the award again yesterday at a rice fair in Hong Kong, which was attended by many rice-producing countries, especially the country. In Asia and countries that import rice.
He added that 27 rice exporters from Cambodia participated in the rice fair, and six types of Cambodian rice were on display to compete for the award.
The award-winning rice is Cambodian jasmine rice.
Since then, Cambodia's rice exports have increased significantly.
New Siem Reap International Airport to Start in 2014
The new Siem Reap International Airport (NSRIA) will begin construction in early 2014.
This is according to Mr. Tek Reth Samrech, Chairman of the Airport Board of Directors.
The new Siem Reap International Airport was once expected to begin groundbreaking in May this year, but missed out.
Tek Reth decided that the $ 1 billion investment project was proceeding normally.
Mr. Reth decided:
They travel up and down to do this work.
Regarding the delay in hitting the ground, Mr. Tek Reth decided to explain that because the bank in Korea, which is a supporter of the new airport project, faced a budget crisis in 2012, which required the investment company to restructure the budget, but added That both investment companies are accelerating work on the project in 2013.
Assets held in the bank to support the airport project, along with the Camko City real estate project, were handed over to the Korean government-owned insurance corporation in 2011.
Tek Reth Samrech says the project process is not slow:
So far, it has only been three years.
The development is scheduled to take place in two districts, Chi Kreng and Sotnikum, 60 kilometers from the provincial town of Siem Reap.
Both companies have already invested $ 200 million to buy land for construction.
Commodity prices rise as customs strengthen
Consumers have begun to pay higher prices for consumer goods after the government ordered the General Department of Customs and Excise to strengthen customs revenue collection and strengthen governance.
Experts say rising daily commodity prices will affect already low-income Cambodians.
Hirami, director general of a company that imports and distributes a wide range of food products and daily necessities, said prices such as food, beverages and other products have risen 15 to 20 percent recently. After strengthening and restricting the payment of taxes from the customs already.
In the past, the influx of goods from Thailand has been largely taxed, mostly through informal subcontracting to customs officials at the border.
At a cabinet meeting on October 25, Prime Minister Hun Sen ordered the General Department of Customs and Excise to adjust its work to two months.
On November 13, the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia issued guidelines on strengthening the effectiveness of customs clearance.
The directive, signed by Mr. Pen Simon, Director General of the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia, stated that the General Department of Customs observed some inactivity and discrimination in relation to the provision of customs clearance procedures. Affect the business environment and fair competition in a free market economy.
Im Mao, a trader at Kab Ko Market who sells children's clothing, women's clothing and other daily necessities, said that almost all goods have risen in price in the last few weeks. This makes sales very difficult.
Cambodia spent about $ 6.8 billion on imports in the first nine months of this year, up 13 per cent from $ 6 billion in the same period last year.
For many imported goods, especially food, beverages and other daily necessities, Cambodia imports from neighboring countries such as Vietnam and Thailand.
Tran Tum, a trade officer at the Vietnamese Embassy in Cambodia, acknowledged that he had observed that the price of Cambodian goods imported from Vietnam was rising, but not because of the price increase from Vietnam, but because of the new policy. The General Department of Customs in Cambodia, which makes it difficult to import goods.
Srey Chanthy, president of the Cambodian Economic Association, said that in this situation, balancing the fight against corruption and controlling inflation was a difficult decision.
He said the government should lower import tariffs on essential goods for daily consumption and instead increase tariffs on other high-end products such as electronics.
Chea Serey, director general of the National Bank of Cambodia, acknowledged the recent rise in market prices.
She said the increase was due to delays in the supply of goods at border crossings as importers had to pay a full 100 per cent customs duty, which pushed up prices.
She said revenue from customs duties would help increase state revenue to pay for infrastructure and civil servants' salaries.
Cambodia may be short of salt next year
Cambodia may face a shortage of salt supply in 2014 due to weather problems.
This is according to the president of the Kampot Salt Producers Association.
Ly Seng, president of the Kampot Salt Producers Association, said that if salt production in 2014 fell further, Cambodia could have to import salt from abroad.
In 2009, Cambodia produced only 30,000 tonnes of salt, which required the import of 60,000 tonnes of salt from China to supply the market, and this was the first time Cambodia had imported salt from China.
According to Ly Seng, the total area of 4,500 hectares of farmers in the association can yield 90,000 tons of salt per season.
In general, salt farmers will start production in early January and last harvest between March and April.
Nuon Phalla, a farmer with 80 hectares of salt fields capable of producing 1,300 tonnes of salt, said he was preparing to start production.
He is not sure if it will be able to produce as planned due to the changing climate.
Regarding the expansion of salt production, the president of the Salt Producers Association says the government has also encouraged farmers to increase production.
The association submitted a proposal to the government four months ago for an additional 200 hectares to meet growing demand.
Mr. Seng said the company had leased land from farmers on 120 hectares of 4,500 hectares and promised that the harvested salt would be returned to the association if the association's salt production was not enough for the local market.
Experts urge MFIs to provide loan insurance
MFI Cambodia should step up its provision of loan insurance services to avoid foreclosure by accidental clients and to reduce its debt risk.
According to experts in the microfinance industry.
Speaking at a seminar on the Rural Entrepreneurship Development Program for Cambodia held in Phnom Penh yesterday, Jaime Aristotle, Managing Director and Founder of CARD MRI, a Philippine-based financial services institute, said: He said that Cambodian microfinance institutions should promote the provision of loan insurance services to protect their clients in the event of an incident that results in loss of life or disability.
MFI loan insurance is a program to help repay debts that customers have borrowed from MFIs in the event of an accident that leads to loss of life or permanent disability. Make money.
Thus, by purchasing loan insurance, customers can avoid foreclosure in case of loss of life and unpaid debt.
Maternity Insurance started operating on this project by piloting the service by providing services to the customers of Sami Institution in 2007 and then officially running until today.
Many large microfinance institutions in Cambodia are also interested and are preparing to launch this service.
VisionFund Cambodia has been partnering with KPMI micro-life insurance company since the end of 2012 and is currently providing health insurance services to its clients.
Hattha Kaksekar Microfinance Institution is also in the process of piloting the provision of this micro-insurance service to its clients.
Cam Sary, CEO of Maternal Insurance Company, said that the promotion of micro-life insurance and loan insurance services to customers is very important because it is a tool to reduce the risk for customers who receive loans from the institution. Microfinance will avoid foreclosure.
In 2011, PRASAC MFI piloted the provision of this service to its clients in collaboration with general insurance company Forte, but also suspended it.
