Tourists rush to provinces while Phnom Penh is quiet
While the Water Festival is canceled for the third time this year, most tourists are touring provincial resorts.
During the three-day holiday, many national tourists visit the country's beaches and historical sites.
Tourism officials say guesthouses and hotels in Cambodia's historic sites are full.
Food prices in the coastal areas have risen by about 10 to 20 percent.
Chhey Chhorn, director of the Siem Reap provincial tourism department, said that during the three days, Siem Reap received a total of 63,016 national and international tourists, an increase of nearly 17 per cent from the same period last year.
He added that to celebrate this year's Water Festival, the community of Chong Kneas, a floating village in Siem Reap, organized a two-day fishing boat race for two days, but did not attract many visitors.
Another historical site, the Temple of Preah Vihear, also attracts many tourists.
Figures from Mr. You Sovann, Deputy Director of the Preah Vihear Provincial Department of Tourism, show that the number of tourists visiting the Temple of Preah Vihear increased to 400 per day for three days, which on a normal day only visitors. Only 100 people.
He said that in Preah Vihear province, there were about 400 hotel and guesthouse rooms, and that over the past three days, about 80 per cent of the rooms had been rented out.
Sihanoukville is still a popular beach destination for local tourists.
During the three-day break, Sihanoukville welcomed a total of 71,517 guests, up 27 percent from the previous year.
Concerts by local television stations added to the bustle of the beach.
Mr. Seng Kha, Director of the Preah Sihanouk Provincial Department of Tourism, explained that the reason for this remarkable increase in tourists was due to the good condition of the road to Sihanoukville, as well as the beautiful beaches and adequate accommodation services in Province.
Kampot has also become a popular beach destination.
The province, which is rich in natural attractions, attracts 28,900 tourists.
Phnom Bokor attracted 19,500 visitors over the three days.
In addition, tourists go to other tourist destinations in the province.
Soy Kanol, director of the Kampot Provincial Department of Tourism, said that Kampot did not have any major events to celebrate the three-day Water Festival, but the department had organized security and strengthened tourism services among vendors and Tour Operators.
Vietnam market closed to wholesale market
Vietnamese supermarkets along Monivong Boulevard have been closed as Vietnamese trade officials claim that as a result of investment, the market is turning to a wholesale market.
A Vietnamese wholesale market is being built on the outskirts of Phnom Penh in Russey Keo district to promote Vietnamese products in Cambodia.
The Vietnamese supermarket opened in late December 2010 with a Vietnamese-Cambodian company, Seng Meng, which owns Z38 and is a member of the Vietnam Business Association in Cambodia.
Tran Tu, deputy trade officer at the Vietnamese embassy in Phnom Penh, said the wholesale market was limited to selling large quantities of goods to Cambodian customers, rather than just retailing to promote Vietnamese products in the market.
The wholesale market is located on National Road 5, just 9 km from Phnom Penh.
Tu says the area is 10 hectares, but the warehouse will be built on one hectare only due to limited investment capital.
Now they have found it.
This partner provides land for construction.
This is the first investment.
So I think it is good for those who create a wholesale market like this in Cambodia.
But I think that takes time.
Mr. Seng Meng declined to comment on the project.
