Cambodia's crocodile exports to end
Crocodile farmers have turned to raising crocodiles for skins after the business of selling crocodiles struggled to find a market, and the supply of skins showed high potential.
Since the end of 2012, the Siem Reap Crocodile Breeding Association has turned its business of raising children into skins, exporting about 20,000 crocodile skins to Thailand.
The association is currently negotiating with buyers in China to export crocodile skin from Cambodia to the Chinese market.
Experts say Cambodia's crocodile skin market has great potential.
Heng Sovannara, director of the crocodile development section of the General Department of Fisheries Administration, said crocodile farmers would earn more if they turned to skins instead of exporting crocodiles.
Heng Sovannara said Cambodia had tried to export crocodile skins for the first time in 2012 and has since pushed two crocodile-raising associations in Siem Reap to start raising skins.
The Cambodian Crocodile Breeding Development Association of Siem Reap was the first exporter of skins in November 2012, which has so far exported 20,000 skins to Thailand.
The association has more than 100 crocodile farms.
Sen Rith, vice president of the Cambodian Crocodile Breeding Development Association in Siem Reap, claims that the supply of Cambodian crocodile skins to overseas markets is much lower than demand.
Our association has just over 20,000 themes during this period.
Sen Rith also owns a crocodile farm.
To increase the supply of crocodile skins, a new business in Cambodia, another Siem Reap Angkor crocodile breeding association was established in early 2012 with 110 members.
With 10 years of experience in the crocodile business, Visal sees that being able to produce high standard crocodile skins is still a challenge for Cambodian breeders.
He said a lack of breeding techniques and investment capital was the main obstacle to producing standard quality skins.
Heng Sovannara explained the delay in exporting crocodile skins, saying that most crocodile farmers could not afford to wait for large crocodiles and did not have enough capital to set up standard breeding grounds.
To date, 357 crocodile farms have been registered with the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries.
About 50,000 crocodiles were exported from Cambodia this year.
The Cambodian Crocodile Breeding Development Association in Siem Reap expects to produce 100,000 crocodile skins for export next year.
The association is also ready to expand supplies to China in the next two years if negotiations between China and Cambodia are successful.
Three-day business is quiet
Business conditions along the riverfront and at other destinations frequented by people for the Water Festival seem to have calmed down for three days.
