In 2011, the government suspended the celebrations for the first time due to the flood crisis.
The suspension in 2012 was due to share in the grief of the death of Samdech Norodom Sihanouk.
Ms. Lin said the Water Festival should be repeated and celebrated if next year there are no bad incidents.
Exports to US increase slightly in 9 months
Cambodia's exports to the United States in the first nine months of the year rose 3.7 per cent from the same period last year.
This is according to figures from the US Department of Commerce.
Factory officials and representatives see the small increase as a result of workers 'strikes, while unions say increasing workers' freedoms is the key to better production.
In the first nine months of this year, Cambodia's total exports to the United States totaled $ 2,092 million, compared to $ 2,177 million last year.
Imports from the United States amounted to $ 173 million, down 3.8 percent from $ 180 million in the same period last year.
Kong Putheara, a spokesman for the Ministry of Commerce, said political issues and workers' strikes could lead investors to divert their attention from Cambodia to Myanmar's new rival in the region.
They will leave Cambodia for Myanmar.
At least one person was killed in violence between SL factory workers and police last week.
Cheat Khemara, a senior official with the Garment Manufacturers Association of Cambodia (GMAC), attributed the slowdown in exports to the United States due to instability in labor relations between workers and employers, as well as post-election political instability.
Workers spend time on strike demanding higher wages than the law, leading to lower productivity.
Unscheduled shipments cause factories and buyers to worry about losses.
Now the buyer places the order from us.
Political instability also leads to workers, investors and buyers feeling anxious and unsettled.
Some trust was taken from Cambodia to other countries.
Kong Athit, vice-president of the Coalition of Cambodian Garment Workers' Democratic Union, explained that the increase in exports did not depend on workers alone.
He said that providing better working conditions and freedoms to workers and negotiating a win-win solution between workers and employers is a guarantee for effective production.
Independent economist from the Cambodian Economic Association, Chanthy, said the small increase in Cambodian exports to the United States would not have a serious impact on the Cambodian economy.
However, he said the supply chain from Cambodia could slow down if the political crisis and workers' strikes remain unresolved.
Strikes can also damage the reputation of their clothing brand.
The United States is Cambodia's leading export destination after Europe.
The main Cambodian products exported to the United States are clothing and footwear.
Cambodia imports cars, machinery, equipment and supplies from the United States.
The two countries' trade volume last year totaled $ 2.92 billion, up 0.7 percent from the same period last year.
Data from the Ministry of Commerce show that Cambodia's total exports of goods worth $ 5.2 billion in the first nine months of this year, up 26 percent from $ 4.1 billion in the same period last year. .
Cambodia may be affected by US measures
Cambodia has maintained 7% economic growth this year despite the floods and political crisis.
However, the IMF said it could be affected in the near future by the US monetary easing policy, which could slow growth.
Speaking at a press conference on Cambodia's economic outlook in Phnom Penh last Friday, Meral Karasulu, the IMF's vice president for Asia and the Pacific, said the planned suspension of US monetary easing was planned. In the future, it may affect Cambodia.
US policy may affect cash flows in Asia, including Cambodia, which will affect the flow of foreign direct investment, FDI to Cambodia, tourism and foreign banking services.
She was referring to the indirect impact of future US liquidity cuts in the US market.
The International Monetary Fund (IMF) has forecast that Cambodia will maintain 7% economic growth in 2013, revising the agency's April forecast of 6.7%.
The US monetary policy has been in place since late 2008 in response to the global economic crisis through bank debt collection.
This policy increases the amount of cash in the market, allowing banks to expand their lending rates at reasonable interest rates to encourage investment activity.
But the US Federal Reserve said in June it would consider reducing bond purchases, meaning that cash flow in markets would be reduced.
Mey Kalyan, an adviser to the Supreme Economic Council of Cambodia, said US monetary policy could reduce cash flow in regional markets.
