Deposits at MFIs Decline
MFIs in Cambodia received a total of $ 365 million in deposits as of the end of September 2013, down nearly 4 per cent from the previous three months to the end of June.
The drop in deposits was due to an increase in the number of customers who withdrew in July and August due to concerns over the post-election political uncertainty.
This is according to experts in the microfinance industry in Cambodia.
Savings in the industry fell from $ 378 million at the end of June to $ 365 million at the end of the month, according to an update from the Cambodian Association of Microfinance Institutions (CMA) released last weekend. Miss.
Mr. Bun Mony, President of the Association of Microfinance Institutions of Cambodia and CEO of Sathapana Microfinance Institution, said that the uncertain situation of the post-election political events has had some effect on consumer confidence, which has led to the size of deposits. Microfinance institutions are declining.
Obtaining savings from local customers is a source of capital for microfinance institutions in Cambodia to reduce their reliance on loans from foreign lenders.
The National Bank of Cambodia has allowed qualified microfinance institutions in Cambodia to accept deposits from clients that microfinance institutions in many countries cannot.
So far, seven major microfinance institutions have received deposit-taking licenses from the National Bank of Cambodia and have been accepting deposits from clients.
Mey Kalyan, an adviser to the Supreme Economic Council of Cambodia, said the reduction in deposits from local customers as lending continued to increase would make lending institutions more dependent on foreign sources. Country and this factor causes cash flow from domestic.
According to a report from the CMA, along with the growth rate of loans in the microfinance industry in Cambodia, each institution also maintains good customer repayment rates.
The amount of non-performing loans was nearly $ 3 million, or 0.3 percent of total loans.
Mr. Bun Mony said the drop in deposit rates was short-lived and he expected the situation to return to normal from now on.
