Mondulkiri: For the Pnong in Cambodia's northeastern province of Mondulkiri, livestock is an important part of the local economy.
Pigs, chickens, cows and buffaloes provide diversity for food and agricultural work.
But elephants are the main bringers of foreign visitors to the area, and foreign visitors are also the bringers of money there.
Mnach Hong, 32, who has been an elephant since he was 13 in Putang village near Sen Monorom, said:
Hong and other local tour guides said tourists' dollars flowed into the community until 2005, when an NGO called the Elephant Valley EVP project began to provide opportunities for tourists. Visit there.
Hong, as well as many other elephants in the area, were affected by the project.
Many tourists now visit the Elephant Valley project site because they say do not go to the community.
It costs $ 60 for two people to travel slowly from the village to the waterfall through the forest and then back to the village.
According to Hong, the business is down 50 percent.
The EVP website advertises full-day trips, including food for $ 70.
After ascending one of the three elephant-designated valleys led by the Himalayas, visitors get a chance to see nature tours and river baths.
In the afternoon, tourists can help bathe the elephants.
She says the project pays local elephants $ 125 a month to bring their elephants to transport tourists so that they do not have to take the elephants out to the wildlife sanctuary.
Although this is a good intention, outrage is growing as local people perceive that the tourist dollars of NGOs are more lucrative compared to local tourism businesses.
Mondulkiri is one of four provinces, including Kratie, Ratanakkiri and Stung Treng, which the Ministry of Tourism is promoting as a nature tourism destination.
Despite concerns that EVP is diverting funds from local people, Mondulkiri has seen a steady increase in the number of domestic and international tourists, up 9.7 percent year-on-year. 2012.
Poch Sorya, deputy director of the Mondulkiri provincial tourism department, believes that the number of tourists who want to ride elephants has actually increased.
But when it comes to local people, they say different things.
Samnang, the owner of Green House, which offers overnight accommodation and tour arrangements, estimates that 80 per cent of tourists ask for an EVP and ignore other options.
Locals call him a double-faced person because he is the only tour operator in town that organizes EVP tours and elephant rides.
"You have also lost the benefit of an overnight stay," said Than Baragech, a local tour guide and tour operator who has been collaborating with the Pnong community for more than eight years.
He said that the elephant ride in Phnong village gave the money directly to the elephants, instead of providing it through a third party.
There are many lives and activities of the people that need to be taken care of, not only for the elephants, but also for the people of Phnong and Cambodia.
