Cassava yields to decline due to floods
Floods are threatening Cambodia's cassava crop, which could reduce yields from last year, as preliminary reports from major cassava-growing provinces show that between 25 and 30 per cent of arable land has been inundated for more than a week. .
Experts predict that the effects of severe floods in the northwestern provinces of Cambodia will reduce the amount of potatoes this year and also affect the livelihoods of many farmers.
Ms. Chanthy, President of the Cambodian Economic Association and an expert in agriculture, said:
The remaining crop is also difficult to compensate for the cost of production.
Cassava is the second most important agricultural crop after rice in terms of cultivated area.
According to statistics from the Ministry of Agriculture, the total area under rice in 2012 was 2.9 million hectares, followed by cassava with a total cultivated area of 337,000 hectares.
The main cassava-growing provinces in Cambodia are Battambang, Banteay Meanchey and Pailin, which are being severely affected by the floods.
Cassava crops may begin to be damaged if flooded for 5 days or more.
Preliminary reports from the provincial agriculture departments of Battambang, Banteay Meanchey and Pailin, obtained by the Post yesterday, show that cassava cultivation in Banteay Meanchey province was affected by floods, with about 25 percent of Battambang's total arable land affected by 28 percent; Pailin province accounts for 25% of total production.
Heng Bunhor, director of the Banteay Meanchey provincial agriculture department, said that the total area under cassava cultivation in the province was 47,100 hectares, of which 12,000 hectares were affected, while another 847 hectares were completely damaged.
Of the 61,000 hectares of cassava plantations in Battambang province, about 17,000 hectares were affected, most of which were flooded for several days and were difficult to restore.
Mr. Chhim Vachira, director of the Battambang Provincial Department of Agriculture, said:
It is difficult to recover because the harvest is near.
Chev Tao, a farmer in Banteay Meanchey province, said his five hectares of farmland had been affected by floods out of more than 50 hectares.
Despite some damage from this year's floods, he claims he will continue to grow cassava next year as it is difficult to find alternative crops because his land does not have irrigation.
