Tourists to Preah Vihear temple decline
Preah Vihear: The total number of tourists visiting the Preah Vihear temple dropped by 30 per cent in the first nine months of this year compared to the same period last year.
A senior official at the provincial tourism department told the Post on Wednesday that a total of about 54,000 tourists had visited the temple during the period, down from 77,450.
Figures show that domestic tourists fell 36 percent to 45,900.
You Sovann, deputy director of the Preah Vihear provincial tourism department, told the Post:
It has been popular for many years for local tourists, so they do not seem to want to visit as before, and we have a lot of new destinations across the country, which is why the decline. .
He added that the increase in foreign tourists was due to the attractiveness of the temple, which has been listed as a World Heritage Site since it was hundreds of years old.
He added that tourists also wanted to see the real situation here after a brief clash with Thai troops.
Clashes erupted at the Preah Vihear temple between Cambodian and Thai troops after UNESCO declared the temple a World Heritage Site, but Thailand claimed control of 4.6 square kilometers. Near the temple.
Cambodia took the dispute to the International Court of Justice in The Hague in April 2011 and is expected to rule on whether Cambodia or Thailand will control the land later this year.
