As the cyclo dwindled, so did the demand for cyclo repair shops, and businesses that relied on repairs stopped operating or began repairing motorcycles instead.
Loam Im Sambath, director of the Cambodian Cyclo Conservation Association, said the decline could be due in part to erosion and fragmentation.
According to Im Sambath, the cyclo, which is still in use, will be more expensive to repair and finding spare parts will be more difficult.
