Rice exports rise sharply
Data from the One-Stop-Shop Secretariat for rice export formalities show that of the total export volume, fragrant and plain rice exports were similar, with one-half share.
During the first nine months, Cambodia exported 123,286 tonnes of fragrant rice, compared to 142,837 tonnes of regular rice.
Kim Savuth, CEO of Khmer Food Rice Exporters and President of the Cambodian Rice Exporters Coalition, said that Cambodia's rice exports this year were better than last year and that many new export markets were booming. Also increased.
Amru Rice Cambodia was the leading exporter with 39,568 tonnes, followed by Khmer Food with 35,519 tonnes and Golden Rice Cambodia with 23,945 tonnes.
At the end of the first half of this year, Cambodia exported a total of 175-959 tonnes of rice, representing 85 per cent of total exports in 2012.
After achieving excellent export results in the first half, the Cambodian government has set a target of exporting 500,000 tonnes this year, or half of its planned export target. At least 1 million tons per year from 2015.
Van David Vichet, deputy secretary-general of the Cambodian Rice Producers and Exporters Association (ARPEC), said the target of exporting 500,000 tonnes this year was unlikely to be achieved due to the deadline. Export with only 3 months left.
He explained that on average we export 30,000 tonnes of rice a month, so in the remaining three months we can export about 90,000 tonnes more.
ADB forecasts 7.2% economic growth
The Asian Development Bank (ADB), based in Manila, Philippines, said in a revision of its 2013 Asian Development Vision released yesterday, a revision of the vision released six months ago.
In 2014, economic growth is projected to reach 7.5 per cent due to the economic recovery in Europe and the United States, Cambodia's main markets.
The ADB's vision contradicts many analysts and officials' comments about the impact of the post-election instability.
Former Commerce Minister Cham Prasidh said in August that opposition protests against the election results could hamper growth.
A recent study by research firm Indochina shows that confidence among Cambodia's foreign trade leaders has fallen by about 50 per cent in the post-election tense period.
Given the impact of the political situation, it is difficult to predict the impact on the current economic process.
According to the ADB's vision, garment and footwear exports to the US and the EU reached $ 2.3 billion in the first six months compared to the same period last year. Is up 11.3 percent.
Rice exports doubled to $ 122 million, while construction project approvals totaled $ 1.9 billion.
Tourism, meanwhile, grew 19.1 per cent year-on-year, with 2.1 million tourists visiting Cambodia from January to June.
The ADB forecasts the service sector as a whole will grow by about 7% this year.
Unexpected slowdowns in Indonesia, Malaysia and Thailand are dampening forecasts for economic performance in Southeast Asia.
The 10 member economies of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) are projected to grow 4.9 percent this year, lower than the 2012 forecast.
Cambodia will not be affected by the slowdown in emerging countries such as China and India.
