Japan plans to ease visa requirements for Cambodia
Japan plans to ease visa requirements for Cambodia to boost tourism for economic growth
This is according to the Japanese Kyodo newspaper, quoting a source from the Japanese government.
The source told Kyodo that the plan would be made later this year and was also considered for Myanmar and Laos.
The Japanese government is considering issuing multi-entry visas for tourists from all three countries.
Ang Kim Eang, president of the Cambodian Association of Travel Agents, said the relaxation of visa laws could help increase the number of Cambodian tourists visiting Japan.
Japanese government statistics on international tourist arrivals do not include Cambodia.
According to figures by the Cambodian Ministry of Tourism, 179,327 Japanese tourists visited Cambodia last year, up 10.8 percent from 2011.
Figures from the Japan External Trade Organization show that trade between Cambodia and Japan increased by 347 million US dollars in the first six months of 2013, up 19 percent from the same period last year. From last year.
Floods do not seriously affect rice
Phnom Penh: The Department of Agriculture in some major rice-producing provinces said yesterday that the ongoing flood situation in those provinces did not cause serious damage to rain-fed rice production this year due to The affected cultivated area is small compared to the total production area.
Heng Bunhor, director of the Banteay Meanchey provincial agriculture department, said the flood situation affecting rice paddies in Banteay Meanchey province was still small compared to the total area.
But he said that not all affected areas were damaged because if the water could recede soon, it would help increase yields.
Battambang province, which has a total area of 279,313 hectares of rain-fed rice, was not affected by the current flood situation.
Chhim Vachira, director of the Battambang Provincial Department of Agriculture, told the Post yesterday that rice and cassava production, the main crops in the province, had not been threatened by floods.
The Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology on Tuesday warned three provinces, including Stung Treng, Kratie and Kampong Cham, to be on high alert as the Mekong River continues to rise for another two to three days.
However, the area under rice cultivation in the three provinces seems to have been slightly affected compared to the total area under cultivation as the water level has begun to recede.
This is according to the director of the provincial agriculture department.
About 5,538 hectares of rice paddies in Stung Treng province were affected out of a total of more than 27,000 hectares.
Of the 32,300 hectares of farmland in Kratie province, 3,600 hectares of farmland in Kratie province were affected by the floods.
Sim Thavirak, deputy director of the Kampong Cham provincial agriculture department, said the impact of the Mekong River floods was still small compared to the province's total cultivated area.
Statistics from the Ministry of Agriculture show that rice cultivation in the 2013 rainy season by the end of August achieved about 95 percent of the annual plan.
