We will no longer be the bride of a poor family that no man wants to marry.
Cambodia asks Japan to support $ 100 million project
Siem Reap provincial authorities have requested support from the Japanese government for a $ 100 million project to build a special economic zone and some key infrastructure to further attract tourists.
Siem Reap Governor Khim Bunsong requested financial support from the Japanese government during a meeting between authorities and officials at the Japan Development Institute (JDI) last Thursday to develop several projects in Siem Reap to attract visitors. More national and international tourists.
He said that about 38 kilometers of damaged roads around Angkor would soon be rehabilitated and were also part of the project.
He added that authorities wanted to install 1 megawatt solar systems and other development plans focused on green tourism in the World Heritage site.
In addition, another major issue that requires the support of the Japanese government is the expansion of National Road 6, which is about 17 kilometers long from Prasat Bakong district to Siem Reap.
The area is located in Slor Kram commune, which is expected to provide more than 3,000 jobs for Siem Reap residents.
Khim Bunsong Construction will begin in 2014 and the money will be used for various development plans, including a museum and a cultural village.
A resident, who asked not to be named, said it was good to ask for money from abroad, but the income from tourism itself should be enough to develop his province.
He added that development seemed too slow for the city's infrastructure.
Data from the Ministry of Tourism shows that the number of tourists arriving in Siem Reap increased by 6% in the first half of this year to 1,122,245 compared to the same period last year.
Livelihoods improved by organic farming
Phnom Penh: More than five years after Huot Thoeun, a farmer living in Phnom Sruoch district, Kampong Speu province, practiced organic farming, his livelihood and living conditions have improved significantly compared to other farmers. .
At that time, many farmers in the village were practicing simple ways to increase rice yields through the use of chemical fertilizers.
Organic farming requires more care and diligence than the use of chemicals.
Farmers must avoid using chemical fertilizers and pesticides to kill insects, instead using compost from natural waste around the house and manure.
In addition, the production process from the beginning to the harvest is done with the right scale, which is more complicated than normal production.
After receiving his first year's yield, Thoeun began to expand his organic rice cultivation area, and by 2010 he had a total of three and a half hectares of organic rice cultivation.
From 2010 until now, farmer Huot Thoeun has continued to cultivate more than 3 and a half hectares of land, which is suitable for the family labor force.
The average annual yield from the total rice harvest is 14 tons.
Organic rice is more expensive than regular rice, and Thoeun can sell jasmine organic rice for 1,300 riel per kilogram, while chemical rice costs 1,000 riel per kilogram.
Revenue from the sale of organic rice has improved Huot Thoeun's life.
As part of increasing raw materials for composting, he bought more cows to raise manure and now has 20 cows.
The farmer, who has four children in his care, can afford to send all of his children to school, and the eldest is studying law at a university in Phnom Penh.
Farmer Huot Thoeun's success is part of a series of changes in the farming practices of many farmers in other provinces who are now gradually turning away from chemical farming and replacing it with methods. Organ.
According to agricultural experts, Cambodia is a potential location for building reputation through the cultivation and export of organic rice, as the rate of population using chemicals to increase rice yields remains high. Still low compared to neighboring countries.
Yang Saing Koma, President of CEDAC, said that since CEDAC encouraged and supported farmers to practice organic farming in 2004, farmers' awareness and the number of farmers who have applied this method have steadily increased.
So far, a total of more than 5,000 families have applied organic farming, of which more than 1,000 have practiced for more than three years.
As of the end of August this year, Cambodia, through CEDAC, had exported about 250 tonnes of organic rice to international markets, most of it to the United States.
The application of organic methods makes farmers pay less but have more benefits.
As the international market becomes more familiar with Cambodia's organic rice and the trend of exporting this type of rice is increasing, it seems to be a stronger incentive for farmers to focus on growing organic rice to improve their living conditions. They.
