Due to declining working conditions in Cambodia's garment sector, the UN-backed BFC Better Factories program will make its first public appearance to deviate from international labor standards.
From January, BFC will publish quarterly online reports on the exports of more than 450 garment factories that have received two or more evaluations listed in the program.
However, the report raises concerns that buyers may turn to other countries.
Buyers do their own audits, they have existing business relationships that could be threatened, he said.
Public disclosure is likely to disrupt the relationship, which could limit the change that buyers are already experiencing the impact.
Assessments on the plant have been underway since October and will be made public in January.
Many factories will be given a chance before the report is released to clear their activities before an official assessment is released online.
According to the BFC, the criteria are in line with Cambodian labor law and international labor standards.
Those criteria include fair pay, child labor, freedom of expression and fire safety.
Jill Tucker, chief technical adviser to the International Labor Organization's Better Factories Cambodia project, said Cambodia now needed to take steps to boost its reputation at a time when major producers were tightening their grip. Tighten controls as well.
