Keat Chhon, Minister of Economy and Finance, said after the reception of four container scanners worth nearly $ 14 million from China yesterday that Cambodia could face a shortage of workers in the garment sector. Affect the national economic growth after the election day, which both parties are competing to win.
The construction and industrial sectors were hampered by labor shortages and agriculture.
However, he claimed that the problem may not seriously affect the economy, which depends on four main sectors: agriculture, garment industry, exports, construction and services and tourism.
Diversification is in the agricultural sector.
According to the Ministry of Economy and Finance, the main components of economic growth were agriculture, which grew 4.2 percent, of which crops grew 4.1 percent, fisheries and aquaculture grew 7.1 percent, and industry grew 9.3 percent. The manufacturing sector grew by 7 percent, electricity, gas and clean water by 8.5 percent and the construction sector by 17.2 percent.
Yang Saing Koma, president of the Cambodian Center for Agricultural Studies and Development (CEDAC), said the rice export policy also contributed to the economy, including high-value exports and job creation.
Son Chhay, chairman of the Cambodia National Rescue Party (CNRP), said that Cambodia's economy was unsustainable because the Cambodian government had in the past borrowed more than $ 1 billion to develop poor infrastructure, such as China. One year.
He said that the Cambodian economy will face serious problems arising from industry and tourism because tourists will not come to Cambodia and workers are afraid not to come to work out of fear because of the large number of troops moving today.
Live pig prices start to rise as imports fall
The price of domestic live pigs has been rising since early August as imports from Vietnam were reduced.
However, large farm owners say prices may fall one day as the Cambodian market relies on neighboring countries.
Ly Laville, director of the Mong Reththy Group pig farm, said yesterday that the price of live pigs had risen from more than 8,000 riel to 9,500 riel per kilogram.
He said the increase in the price of live pigs was due to increased demand for pork from China, which prompted Vietnam to divert imports to China and reduce imports to Cambodia.
He said rising prices in Thailand were also boosting the pig market.
Other reasons may be that local supply does not meet demand, as smallholder farmers abandon breeding during the fall in pig prices, and traders who used to import more from Vietnam also cut back on imports later in the day. Vote.
Thus, immediately after the election, imports fell while Vietnamese pigs rose sharply due to demand in China.
Reliance on foreign markets has destabilized domestic supply.
High imports have hurt local farmers, discouraging farmers from raising them, but when imports are reduced, local supply is in trouble.
Currently, most local farmers have given up raising pigs due to uncontrolled imports, leading to falling prices.
Ly Laville thinks the price is very reasonable for the local pig business, but it is unsustainable because Cambodia relies on imports of live pigs.
In recent times, Cambodia has only imported pigs from Vietnam due to the low price of Vietnamese pigs.
This China is very cruel when it is used by someone and that country knows the price.
Vietnam produces a lot, if China continues to use Vietnam, prices in Cambodia will continue to be high for a long time.
Srun Pov, president of the Pig Breeding Association in Cambodia and owner of a farm that sells about 200 pigs a month, agrees.
He added that farmers are happy with the latest price because they can earn some profit and it is also part of the farmers' motivation to raise.
He said at least 70 percent of farmers had given up raising pigs.
Only 800 pigs are allowed to be imported per day, but the actual number could be more than a thousand.
Panh Chanthet, president of the Phnom Penh Pig Supply Association, said that although prices had risen, the supply was still around 1,000 a day.
Piracy in Cambodia is high
Only 5 to 10 percent of users use licensed software, but most are international companies or organizations that care about data security, legitimacy, and institutional reputation.
The availability of quality and properly licensed products is closely related to the state of economic development and the living conditions of the people.
Many consumers and local companies are aware of the difficulties of using pirated software, but they find it difficult to pay for licensed products due to the high cost.
Var Ratsan, director of the Department of Intellectual Property at the Ministry of Commerce, said so far respect for intellectual property rights for computer software products remained low in Cambodia.
He said the main contributing factors to preventing the use of duplicate computer software are people's livelihoods in accessing real products, consumer awareness and the ability of relevant officials to carry out this work.
Private sector proposes amendments to tax law
The private sector has asked the Royal Government to amend Cambodia's old fiscal law to keep pace with what they believe will help make investment in Cambodia more competitive ahead of the AEC this year. 2015.
The remarks came during a rare meeting yesterday between more than 100 private sector business leaders, led by Kith Meng, president of the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce, and Kong Vibol, director general of the General Department of Taxation.
Kith Meng called the fiscal law passed more than 10 years ago in Cambodia and was inconsistent with the current economic situation.
He said that with only a year and a half left, Cambodia was preparing to enter the ASEAN Economic Community, so investors needed to do everything possible to be competitive by lowering operating costs.
The National Assembly of Cambodia passed the tax law in 1997.
But Kong Vibol said the law was amended in 2003.
Kong Vibol said the government needed to collect taxes more effectively depending on economic growth, so it could be difficult to keep them low during Cambodia's economic growth.
However, Kith Meng cited the example of Hong Kong, where the government collects less taxes but collects more effectively.
Overseas workers drop by 30%
The number of Cambodian workers going abroad legally dropped by nearly 30 per cent in the first half of this year compared to the same period last year.
Most of the decline was in Thailand and South Korea, which are home to Cambodia's largest legal migrant workers.
According to the Ministry of Labor, there are 125,000 legal Cambodian migrant workers in South Korea, Malaysia, Thailand and Japan, and an estimated $ 200 million a year in remittances.
The decline was due to the government's contribution to improving local working conditions.
