Gambling profits show decline
Profits from slot machines at NagaWorld provided lucrative revenue for the Cambodian gaming industry, boosting revenue by $ 3.7 million and an average of $ 256 per machine per day in the second quarter.
Rice prices fall sharply
Banteay Meanchey: Officials and traders claim that rice prices in the northwestern province are falling sharply as early harvest begins.
Heng Bunhor, director of the Banteay Meanchey provincial agriculture department, said on Monday that farmers had rushed to harvest early-season light rice as floods affected more than 3,000 hectares of early-season rice paddies across Banteay Meanchey province.
He said that at the same time, people are trying to harvest to avoid a lot of damage, and the price of freshly harvested rice has fallen steadily to around 700 riel per kilogram.
He said 2,40 hectares of cassava crops in the province were also affected.
Chao Mey Luy, owner of a rice mill named Santi in Rohal village, Rohal commune, Preah Net Preah district, Banteay Meanchey province, said that the price of rice had dropped significantly and that no traders had rushed to buy it during this time because Thailand had prevented it from importing. Enter from Cambodia.
She added that traders who used to order rice from her mills have suspended and the mills have also suspended operations.
Khong Vun, owner of a rice mill in Mongkul Borei district, said farmers' rice was frozen.
He said that the price of IR rice has dropped significantly because the market for this type of rice, Cambodia does not compete with neighboring countries, especially Vietnam.
Jiranan Vongmongkol, commercial adviser at the Thai Embassy in Cambodia, said that the export of rice to the Thai market was strictly prohibited and that imports had been made in the past.
Challenges of factory production chain
The production chain of some garment factories in Phnom Penh has not yet returned to its original state before the election, as some workers have yet to return to work, leaving some parts stranded.
Ath Thorn, president of the Cambodian Labor Union, told the Post that more than 10 percent of workers had not returned to work after the July 28 national election, as some had Concerns about changes in the political situation.
This is a challenge that can affect the production chain in the factory.
Ken Loo, secretary general of the Garment Manufacturers Association of Cambodia, said yesterday that some factories could face the above challenges, but confirmed that they were not common.
However, he worries that if the political situation remains unclear, returning and current workers could return to their homeland, which could be a major obstacle.
Cambodia Airlines Joint Project Delayed
Stakeholders say joint venture to set up Cambodia Airlines will be postponed for another three months.
An earlier report sent to the stock exchange in late May said that Philippine Airlines would invest $ 10 million in a 49 per cent stake in Cambodia Airlines.
According to the aviation industry website Chaviation, Cambodia Airlines had previously operated between 1997 and 2005 when it was wholly owned by the Royal Group.
David Pearson, a supervisor at Royal Group involved in the Cambodia Airlines project, said yesterday that the process had been delayed as the process of obtaining regulatory approval took longer. Long, but it is not a big deal.
He said it would not affect the launch of the first flight, and he insisted the flight would take place this year.
Cambodia Airlines has entered the domestic exclusive market, which currently has only one Cambodia Angkor Air CAA.
A report by the Aviation Center found in January that the exclusive CAA had raised ticket prices at an unusually high rate, causing domestic air travel to fall by about 15 percent.
Food companies increase production
