Economic growth will be affected
Senior Cambodian officials have warned that Cambodia's economic growth will be affected by the post-election political turmoil.
Minister of Commerce Cham Prasidh said at the opening of the two annual industry fairs yesterday that the Cambodian economy would maintain the same growth as last year without a post-election era.
Cham Prasidh seems to be referring to the possibility of mass protests by the opposition against the election results, which would lead to social instability and serious economic damage.
As we have already seen, when there are demonstrations, there are real demonstrators and opportunists.
When demonstrations break people into shops.
He said the recent economic growth was a result of increased foreign demand and the potential for business opportunities in the country was wide.
He said the government's policy was not to focus on a single export, but to diversify its exports.
Kang Chandararot, director of the Institute for the Study of Development in Cambodia, said that from the experience gained from the last election, investor confidence was not high for the Cambodian market.
He added that if the political crisis continued after the election, orders for textiles from the US market could fall in the garment sector in the event that the United States intervenes in recent political affairs, as Cambodia's major market relies on the US market. .
