Cambodia's exports to Japan increase by 31%
Cambodia exported a total of $ 240 million worth of products to Japan in the first half of this year, up 31 per cent from the same period last year.
Exports to Asean countries in the first half of this year, including Thailand, Vietnam, Singapore and Malaysia, fell sharply by an average of 15 percent, according to data from the Japan External Trade Organization (JETRO). Last year.
However, the total volume of Cambodia's exports is still far smaller than that of other countries in the region.
Ms. Takayoshi Kuromiya, Advisor to the Embassy of Japan in Cambodia, expressed her appreciation for the increase in trade volume between Cambodia and Japan and hoped that this trend would continue to work well.
JETRO data shows that Cambodia's main products exported to Japan include footwear, clothing and electrical machinery, and that Cambodia also imports from Japan machinery for factories, cars, ships and boats.
The top exporters to Japan in the first six months were Malaysia with $ 15 billion, Indonesia with $ 14.8 billion and Thailand with $ 11 billion.
Cambodia is the ninth country in the region to export more to Japan than Laos at $ 61 million, followed by Myanmar at $ 287 million.
Kyoto Kadoho, JETRO's representative in Cambodia, once told the Post that Cambodia's exports to Japan were growing and that Cambodia was now not only exporting garments, but also increasing its exports. With higher value added in advance, such as electrical appliances installed by Japanese companies in Cambodia.
He added that more and more industrial plants are investing in Cambodia to assemble vehicles and prepare to export back to Japan.
Bilateral trade between Cambodia and Japan in the first half of this year reached $ 347 million, up from $ 292 million in the first half of last year.
