Thol launches rice delivery service
Phnom Penh: Toll Royal Railways has launched its first rice delivery service from Phnom Penh to the port of Sihanoukville.
The first shipment began last week with a total of 2,400 tonnes of rice.
This is the company's first shipment of goods from Phnom Penh to the international port since it was officially launched in late 2010, as in the past most of the goods shipped were imported goods. Or goods transported from the provinces to Phnom Penh.
David Kerr, chief executive of Toll Royal, said the start of rice shipments now was an important part of his company's contribution to increasing export productivity through the provision of delivery services. Easier.
As a first step, the company ships rice twice a week.
Each container can hold 24 tons of rice.
Previously, the company only shipped goods from the provinces to the capital.
At the end of 2009, the company started shipping about 9,000 tons of cement per month from Touk Meas district, Kampot province to Phnom Penh, and from the beginning of 2013, the company started to transport 1.2 million liters of fuel every two days from the province. Sihanoukville came to Phnom Penh.
The company also transports 900 tonnes of coal every two days from Sihanoukville to Kampot's Touk Meas district.
Kim Savuth, director general of a large rice exporter with a factory in Phnom Penh that has not yet shipped rice through Thol, has expressed interest in transporting it by rail.
However, he said that the process of transporting trains by train is now more complicated and expensive.
He said the reason was due to the requirement to scan rice exported at the port before export, which saves a lot of time to transfer goods.
If they have a scanner at the dry dock and do the inspections there, it will reduce this complicated process and ultimately save time and money.
He explained that first the rice had to be transported by truck, then from the factory to the dry port.
Upon arrival at the dry port, the rice is transferred from the truck to the train container.
When the train arrives at Sihanoukville port, the rice is transferred from the carriage to the truck again to enter the scanner before being shipped out.
