Business in the Islamic community benefits from Ramadan
The retail business in the Khmer-Muslim community market has benefited greatly from sales as the Muslim holiday of Ramadan begins today.
Many retailers in Chrang Chamres market in Russey Keo district claim that their business seems to be making a lot of money in two days.
Vendor Ly Teang said cooking goods were selling like hot cakes in the last two days before Ramadan.
The trader claimed that he sold about 250 kilograms of noodles, which was only 50 kilograms more than on a normal day, while more than 2,000 duck eggs were sold, compared to 400 to 500 eggs on a normal day.
Osman Hassan, president of the Cambodian Islamic Association in Cambodia, said there were about 600,000 Cambodian-Muslims across the country and that they were always prepared to spend more than usual on the last day of Ramadan.
Form a working group on milk powder
The Cambodian government has set up a technical working group to assess the extent of health concerns after infant formula was found to be toxic.
Minister of Health Mam Bunheng told the Post:
At this time, we have not issued a public announcement.
He said he would wait to see the results from the team.
Sok Sophoan, a housewife with two children in Phnom Penh's Meanchey district's Prek Pra commune, said she heard the news from her husband yesterday morning.
She buys about six cans of Dumix milk for her son every month.
He said his staff was actively working to find a place to recall products from retailers.
Customers can call 012 233 283, which was set up in response to inquiries, to find out if they can return their purchased milk.
Dumex milk powder, which contains clear milk ingredients from Fonterra, New Zealand's largest milk producer, is currently found to have bacteria and is banned in China, Russia and Vietnam.
The Cambodian milk powder distributor, which receives ingredients from Fonterra, is recalling thousands of cans of milk powder as a precautionary measure.
According to the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, Fonterra Dairy announced on Saturday that clear milk containing protein sold to infant formula and other beverages could contain viruses. Causes poisoning and causes severe paralysis, which can be fatal.
The center, based in Atlanta, Georgia, says symptoms in children include fatigue, loss of appetite and crying.
Kong Bo claimed on Monday that the company had collected about 7,000 to 8,000 boxes of milk powder, each containing 12 cans identified as toxic.
However, he confirmed that not all milk powder is in the hands of customers.
As of Monday, some milk powder was in stock, and about 700 to 1,000 boxes at retail outlets were recalled and others were resold to customers.
Employees at Dumex began inspecting retailers yesterday to recall suspicious products.
He said customers can return their products to retailers for a refund.
Bo says both customers and retailers will be able to confirm stock code checks related to toxic milk powder by publishing in local newspapers.
China, the world's second-largest economy, has banned all imports of milk powder from New Zealand.
Cambodia at the time also blocked the import of Chinese dairy products.
A general manager at Dumex infant formula retailer in Phnom Penh, who asked not to be named, said the shop owner was aware of the blacklisted product via email from Dumex Cambodia.
On Monday, Fonterra apologized for the horrific poisoning but denied allegations that it had delayed the release of the information.
