Rice exports continue to rise in July
According to data from the One-Stop-Shop Secretariat for rice exports, Cambodia exported a total of 207,370 tonnes of rice in the first seven months, surpassing last year's total. With only 205-717 tons.
Exports peaked in March at 45,413 tonnes and then fell to a monthly average of 22,000 tonnes until exports rebounded in March, the data showed. June.
Song Saran, president of Amru Rice Cambodia, a leading exporter in the first seven months, said the general increase in rice exports was usually in June, which was a good month. High exportability.
He also claimed that many Cambodian rice exporters were rushing to release stockpiled rice as the new season harvest approached.
The main markets for Cambodian rice exports are mostly to the EU market, which represents about 60% of Cambodia's total market share.
An initiative by the European Union to help less developed countries like Cambodia, called Everything But Arms, allows Cambodian rice to be imported into EU member states duty-free at the same time that rice is imported. From Thailand and Vietnam, a tax of about 175 euros ($ 224) per tonne of rice is levied.
Kim Savuth, CEO of Khmer Food Exporters and President of the Cambodian Rice Exporters Coalition, said that Cambodia's rice exports this year were better than last year and that there were many new export markets. There is also an increase.
Shrimp suppliers are facing problems
Phnom Penh: Lack of technology and capital has made some shrimp farm owners give up raising.
Farm owners supplying shrimp fry for many farmers to abandon have given up hatching, claiming there have been no orders from farmers this year.
Mam Saran, a lobster and fish hatcher in Kampot province's Chhouk district, says he has been hatching lobsters and fish for sale since 2009, but this year he has given up hatching altogether. Only fish.
He observed that most farmers who had ordered shrimp fry had given up raising them because they had tried in the past year but had not received any results.
He explained that the farmer who used to buy baby lobsters to raise did not benefit from this business because he lacked many techniques for raising them.
He says the demand for baby lobsters has only been around for a few years, but after the failure of farmers.
However, he continues to incubate between 1.5 million and 2 million fish for sale this year, as hatching costs less.
