Real economic plan failed in campaign
All political parties vying for parliamentary seats in the upcoming elections rarely talk about their plans for the economy, as it is important to do so now.
For a country that went through war and genocide during the 20th century, Cambodia has made some real progress in the last two decades.
Gross domestic product grew nearly sixfold between the first democratic elections in 1993 and the current one.
As a result, Cambodia is limited to entry into middle-income countries.
But the next phase of Cambodia's development is more risky.
While much of our growth in the past has been supported by tax incentives, low labor costs and abundant natural resources, as well as contributions from the garment and mining industries, it will not last long.
Rising labor costs and controversial industrial relations mean some investors will leave to seek a place with lower wages.
In addition, in a country with modern means of transportation and telecommunications, Cambodians and Cambodian business people will have to deal with difficult issues with their counterparts beyond our borders in finding employment and in finding jobs. Protect the market.
Probably too early to talk about being in the middle-income country.
But the risks to Cambodia's future economic growth will be unpredictable, unsustainable and unstable without real reform and strategic investment in a number of key areas.
For example, the capture of education, the efforts to build an education system after the fall of the Khmer Rouge regime, yielded commendable results in terms of physical infrastructure and enrollment and literacy rates. Letters.
But the fundamental problem remains.
While the curriculum is constantly being improved, the pedagogical base is non-existent.
The four-hour school day is shorter than in neighboring countries, and the inclusion of the center's student curriculum has not been particularly successful.
College education, which many universities focus on, seems to be more about degrees, rather than providing students with practical skills in response to the needs of the job market.
Due to low salaries, the teaching profession has tried to attract and retain young students who want a college degree instead of choosing a high-paying job in the private sector.
Basic education reform should take a long time.
Access to such schooling and textbooks is no longer a major issue, and the emphasis must be on improving the quality of education to ensure that Cambodia's next generation is ready. For a global economy where more jobs, less labor, and more knowledge.
The environment is another area that is receiving more and more attention than it currently has.
According to the Food and Agriculture Organization of Cambodia, Cambodia's main rainforest attraction has fallen sharply from more than 70 percent of its total forest area in 1970 to just 3 percent in 2007. Make deforestation rate in Cambodia among the fastest growing deforestation countries in the world.
Disputes over mining rights and economic land concessions quickly deplete valuable resources that could not be recovered.
Putting Cambodia's economic growth on a steady path requires a reassessment of the impact of these large-scale commercial activities on the environment and the living conditions of the rural poor.
More importantly, Cambodia relies heavily on foreign aid to fund its annual budget.
On the other hand, foreign aid fills a gap in funding for social services such as health and education.
On the other hand, being ready to receive subsidies will reduce incentives to strengthen local revenue collection by expanding tax collection bases and more effective law enforcement measures.
Self-funding is important because it is a stable source of funding for physical and social infrastructure, as well as a level of fiscal responsibility.
These reforms are comprehensive, but they are an important first step towards building a stronger foundation for the future.
