Revenue from Angkor ticket sales higher than government figures
Phnom Penh: According to data from the Apsara Authority on the number of foreign tourists visiting Angkor Wat, calculated by the Phnom Penh Post, the revenue from ticket sales to Angkor Wat is still higher than the figures presented by the Apsara Authority.
The 2012 revenue estimate of $ 51 million is still higher than the government's figure of $ 30 million, while the opposition claims more than $ 150 million a year.
Bun Narith, director general of the Apsara Authority, confirmed to the Post yesterday that the ticket sales were around $ 51 million a year, based on actual data from the number of tickets sold and the number of tickets sold. Ticket price.
We can verify the number of tourists with the private sector working in the tourism sector, such as tour companies and hotels.
A new report released last Friday, signed by Bun Narith, put the total number of foreign tourists visiting Siem Reap at about 2 million, but the number of ticket buyers was about 1 million. 8 million, with some tourists being allowed to visit free of charge.
According to the Post, the number of visitors who bought tickets for the day was 59.2 per cent of the total 1,070,704, according to the Post.
The number of customers who bought tickets for the three-day tour was 39.8 percent, or 719,831, while the number of customers who bought tickets for the week was 1 percent, or 18,086.
Depending on the number of tickets sold and the ticket price, revenue could be around $ 51 million from 2012.
The release of the report is in response to the opposition, which claims nearly $ 200 million in annual revenue.
Kem Sokha, acting president of the Cambodia National Rescue Party, said that revenue from Angkor tickets was nearly $ 200 million a year.
Sok An, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister in Charge of the Office of the Council of Ministers and Chairman of the Apsara Authority, stated on the 5th anniversary of the inclusion of the Temple of Preah Vihear as a World Heritage Site that the revenue from ticket sales for Angkor was only 30 million. Dollars only.
Son Chhay, the CNRP's parliamentary candidate, claimed that the data was fraudulent and did not reflect reality.
He said the actual number of visitors to Angkor could reach more than 2 million, while tickets for the three-day period were higher.
We have made several observations and found that most visitors buy tickets for Angkor for at least three days.
He called for allowing independent research institutions to study actual revenues to strengthen management transparency and increase public confidence.
Previously, the Post reported that revenue from Angkor ticket sales was controlled by the Apsara Authority and Sok Kong's Sokha Hotel.
The first 3 million in revenue must be divided equally between the two parties.
The remaining revenue is allocated as follows: 15 per cent for the development of Angkor, 68 per cent to the Apsara Authority, while 17 per cent to the Sokha Hotel.
Cambodia awaits funding for other GI products
Phnom Penh: The National Committee for Intellectual Property says that while Kampot pepper and Kampong Speu palm sugar have been included as GI markers, new entrants seeking the same benefits are still waiting for funding to enable them. Incorporate other products into this GI as well.
In 2010, Kampot pepper and Kampong Speu palm sugar were the first Cambodian products to be geographically registered and protected under international law.
