This year's tax collection for motorbikes and tuk-tuks can be paid at ACLEDA Bank.
Data from the General Department of Taxation shows that total tax revenue in the first half of this year was $ 470.3 million, up 21.2 percent from the same period last year, which collected $ 388 million. The month was up 24.8 percent, corporate income tax was up 28.6 percent, value added tax was up 12.2 percent and special tax was up 17.5 percent.
Most pig farmers give up because it is not profitable
It is not new for local pig farmers who are facing difficulties due to oversupply of pigs from neighboring countries, while the problem remains unresolved.
Allowing unchecked and well-controlled inflows is hurting local market prices, especially for smallholder farmers.
According to the large farm owners who supply the breeders, most farmers or small farm owners have given up raising pigs because they could not make a profit and can afford the cost.
Ly Laville, director of the Mong Reththy Group pig farm, said that Cambodian authorities had allowed more pigs to be imported, causing local pig prices to fluctuate, and local farmers had abandoned many pig farms.
He added that among smallholder farmers who raised 50 or fewer seedlings, almost all had been abandoned.
The company's market research results in Phnom Penh and Sihanoukville show that the two markets currently need a total of 2,120 pigs per day, of which Phnom Penh needs 2,000 and Sihanoukville 120.
The company plans to sell about 300 breeds of pigs a month, but actual sales do not match that number, and currently sells between 5,000 and 6,000 broilers a month.
Srun Pov, president of the Pig Breeding Association in Cambodia, said that the family pig business has closed down from now on because people are reluctant to raise pigs, which has no real value and is stable and is a thing of the past. Let them lose a lot of money.
To date, at least 70 percent of farmers have given up farming.
Only 800 pigs are allowed to be imported per day, but the actual number could be in the thousands.
Laville says:
For me, the incentive for local farmers is to strictly control imports.
I feed less, it dies less, I feed more, it dies more.
Since 2008, the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries has allowed local private companies to import 800 live pigs per day to meet local demand.
Kao Phal, director of the Ministry of Agriculture's General Department of Production and Veterinary Medicine, hung up when asked questions about the ministry's actions.
