Phnom Penh: A local company, Soma, has begun investing nearly $ 3 million to build a biomass plant aimed at processing waste from rice mills in two districts, Bati district, Takeo province and Stong district in Kampong Thom province. .
According to company officials.
Vong Samang, general manager of Soma Consulting Group, says Soma has purchased technology from US-based GE and India-based energy technology company ANKUR to install 100 husk-powered machines. Percentage for electricity generation.
Now we may need a cooperative partner in this investment.
He said that so far in Cambodia, there are only about 50 rice mills equipped with machines for biomass production, but they are still small-scale and do not use 100% of the husk. 70% husk and 3% diesel, but Soma's biomass machine uses 100% husk.
The company is currently under construction and will complete in early 2014.
Our goal is to use rice husks to generate electricity to supply the rural population.
The biomass plant produces 750 kilowatts per hour and requires between 1.8 and 2 kilograms of husk per kilowatt hour.
Victor Zona, deputy director general for renewable energy at the Ministry of Industry, Mines and Energy, said Cambodia was an agricultural country, so biomass had great potential for rural electricity development.
He said two sugar companies had installed the biomass power plant, one in Kampong Speu and the other in Kratie, generating nearly 50 megawatts per hour.
Cambodia also has nearly 50 small biomass plants in Battambang and Banteay Meanchey provinces.
However, biomass production equipment is still expensive to install.
GI pepper sales fall slightly this year
The Kampot Pepper Association sold 21 tonnes of Kampot pepper as of May this year, of which 70 per cent had been exported.
This is according to data from the president of the association.
Figures show that sales of Kampot pepper, which has received GI certification as a certificate of intellectual property for the best quality of pepper products, decreased from last year's 22 tonnes, which was sold.
The drop in sales this year was due to poor yields due to unfavorable market conditions caused by unfavorable weather.
The pepper harvest season in Kampot is between January and May.
He added that in fact, the demand for Kampot pepper has been increasing every year after the product received GI certification in 2010, but the main problem is the availability of supply.
Favorable geographical location to grow the best quality pepper to get GI, covering 4 districts in Kampot and 2 other districts in Kep.
So far, the Kampot Pepper Association has a total of 153 families and is ready to add 135 more families when their cultivable area can be harvested.
According to the Kampot Pepper Association, there are three types of Kampot pepper, of which the highest price is $ 18 per kilogram, red pepper $ 15 and black pepper $ 11 per kilogram.
She claims that the company has exported six tonnes of Kampot pepper and expects that if the association can supply as much pepper as the company wants, sales will increase even more.
I hope that this production will make a significant contribution to improving the lives of farmers.
In response to the growing demand for Kampot pepper products and to be able to take full advantage of the potential of Kampot pepper, the association members are working to expand the cultivated area.
At present, the area that can be harvested is only 20 hectares, because the newly planted area needs two more years to be harvested.
Sihanoukville: Freight through the Sihanoukville Autonomous Port in the first half increased by more than 15 percent.
Chea Sambath, head of planning and marketing at the port, said the increase in freight was in line with the global economy, the regional economy as well as the country's economy.
It confirms that our regional economy as well as the global economy is growing, so we will see demand grow.
We have a good growth rate, even though it changes every month.
The International Monetary Fund recently forecast that the global economy will grow 3.1 percent in 2013 and 3.8 percent next year.
The data show that most of the exports during this period were garments and some agricultural products, such as acacia processing, wood processing, potatoes and rice.
Most of the imported products are industrial raw materials and construction materials, automobiles and oil.
This shows that the industry is on an upward trend.
Most of our products are exported to Europe, America and Asia, such as the Philippines, Japan, Korea.
